t{PairwiseDisjoint}(f) $, given the directedness condition on $ s $.\n[4] subsingleton_setOf_mem_iff_pairwise_disjoint  (theorem)\n    {f : ι → Set α} : (∀ a, {i | a ∈ f i}.Subsingleton) ↔ Pairwise (Disjoint on f)\n    For a family of sets $ f $, the following are equivalent: (1) For every element $ a $, the set of indices $ i $ such that $ a \\in f(i) $ is a subsingleton (i.e., contains at most one element); and (2) The family $ f $ is pairwise disjoint, meaning $ f(i) $ and $ f(j) $ are disjoint for all distinct indices $ i \\neq j $.\n[5] Set.PairwiseDisjoint  (definition)\n    (s : Set ι) (f : ι → α) : Prop\n    For a set $ s $ and a function $ f $, this proposition holds if for every pair of distinct elements $ x $ and $ y $ in $ s $, the images $ f(x) $ and $ f(y) $ are disjoint in the partially ordered set $ \\alpha $.\n[6] Set.PairwiseDisjoint.sSupIndep  (theorem)\n    ∀ {α : Type u_1} [inst : Order.Frame α] {s : Set α}, s.PairwiseDisjoint id → sSupIndep s\n    In a frame $\\alpha$, if a set $s$ satisfies that every pair of distinct elements are disjoint (i.e., $x \\wedge y = \\bot$ for all distinct $x, y \\in s$), then $s$ is supremum-independent. This means that for each $a \\in s$, the element $a$ is disjoint from the supremum of $s \\setminus \\{a\\}$, i.e., $a \\wedge \\bigsqcup(s \\setminus \\{a\\}) = \\bot$.\n[7] Setoid.IsPartition.pairwiseDisjoint  (theorem)\n    {c : Set (Set α)} (hc : IsPartition c) : c.PairwiseDisjoint id\n    If $ c $ is a partition of a set $ \\alpha $, then all subsets in $ c $ are pairwise disjoint, meaning that for any distinct $ A, B \\in c $, the intersection $ A \\cap B $ is empty.\n[8] Set.PairwiseDisjoint.subset  (theorem)\n    (ht : t.PairwiseDisjoint f) (h : s ⊆ t) : s.PairwiseDisjoint f\n    If $ s $ is a subset of a set $ t $ that satisfies the pairwise disjointness condition under a function $ f $, then $ s $ also satisfies the pairwise disjointness condition under $ f $. Formally, given $ s \\subseteq t $ and $ t $