: ℝ)\n    Given a pseudo-metric space $ \\alpha $, a point $ x \\in \\alpha $, and a non-negative real number $ \\varepsilon $, the closed ball centered at $ x $ with radius $ \\varepsilon $ is the set of all points $ y \\in \\alpha $ such that the distance $ d(y, x) $ is less than or equal to $ \\varepsilon $.\n[3] Finset.pairwiseDisjoint_range_singleton  (theorem)\n    : (Set.range (singleton : α → Finset α)).PairwiseDisjoint id\n    The collection of all singleton finite sets in a type $\\alpha$ is pairwise disjoint, meaning that for any distinct elements $a$ and $b$ in $\\alpha$, the singleton sets $\\{a\\}$ and $\\{b\\}$ have empty intersection.\n[4] Finset.pairwiseDisjoint_coe  (theorem)\n    {ι : Type*} {s : Set ι} {f : ι → Finset α} : s.PairwiseDisjoint (fun i => f i : ι → Set α) ↔ s.PairwiseDisjoint f\n    For a set $ s $ and a function $ f $ mapping elements of $ s $ to finite subsets of $ \\alpha $, the pairwise disjointness of the images under the canonical coercion from finite sets to subsets is equivalent to the pairwise disjointness of the original finite sets. Formally, $ s $-indexed family $ f $ has pairwise disjoint finite sets if and only if the same family, when coerced to subsets of $ \\alpha $, also has pairwise disjoint images.\n[5] Metric.closedBall_disjoint_closedBall  (theorem)\n    (h : δ + ε < dist x y) : Disjoint (closedBall x δ) (closedBall y ε)\n    In a pseudo-metric space, if the sum of non-negative real numbers $ \\delta $ and $ \\varepsilon $ is strictly less than the distance $ d(x, y) $ between two points $ x $ and $ y $, then the closed balls centered at $ x $ with radius $ \\delta $ and at $ y $ with radius $ \\varepsilon $ are disjoint. Formally, $ \\text{Disjoint}(\\overline{B}(x, \\delta), \\overline{B}(y, \\varepsilon)) $, where $ \\overline{B}(z, r) $ denotes the closed ball centered at $ z $ with radius $ r $.\n[6] EMetric.disjoint_closedBall_of_lt_infEdist  (theorem)\n    ∀ {α : Type u} [inst : PseudoEMetricSpace α] {x :