henever $ f(i) \\cap f(j) $ is non-empty, it must hold that $ i = j $.\n[2] OrderedFinpartition.disjoint  (theorem)\n    ∀ {n : ℕ} (self : OrderedFinpartition n), Set.univ.PairwiseDisjoint fun m => Set.range (self.emb m)\n    For any natural number $ n $ and any ordered finite partition of $ n $, the images of the embedding functions for each part form a pairwise disjoint family of subsets of $ n $. In other words, for distinct indices $ m $ and $ m' $, the ranges of the embeddings $ \\text{emb}(m) $ and $ \\text{emb}(m') $ are disjoint sets.\n[3] Set.PairwiseDisjoint.elim_finset  (theorem)\n    {s : Set ι} {f : ι → Finset α} (hs : s.PairwiseDisjoint f) {i j : ι} (hi : i ∈ s) (hj : j ∈ s) (a : α) (hai : a ∈ f i)\n  (haj : a ∈ f j) : i = j\n    For a set $ s $ of indices and a function $ f $ assigning finite sets to each index, if the family $ \\{f(i)\\}_{i \\in s} $ is pairwise disjoint, then any element $ a $ belonging to both $ f(i) $ and $ f(j) $ for $ i,j \\in s $ necessarily implies $ i = j $. This asserts the uniqueness of indices for overlapping elements in pairwise disjoint finite sets.\n[4] Finset.pairwiseDisjoint_slice  (theorem)\n    : (Set.univ : Set ℕ).PairwiseDisjoint (slice 𝒜)\n    For any finite set $\\mathcal{A}$ of finite sets that is pairwise disjoint, the subset of $\\mathcal{A}$ consisting of all elements with cardinality $r$ is also pairwise disjoint. Formally, if $\\mathcal{A} \\subseteq \\mathcal{P}(\\alpha)$ satisfies $A \\cap B = \\emptyset$ for distinct $A,B \\in \\mathcal{A}$, then $\\{A \\in \\mathcal{A} \\mid \\#A = r\\}$ remains pairwise disjoint.\n[5] Finset.pairwise_disjoint_powersetCard  (theorem)\n    (s : Finset α) : Pairwise fun i j => Disjoint (s.powersetCard i) (s.powersetCard j)\n    For any finite set $ s $, the collections of subsets with exactly $ i $ elements and exactly $ j $ elements are disjoint for all distinct natural numbers $ i $ and $ j $. In symbols, $ \\mathcal{P}_i(s) \\cap \\mathcal{P}_j(s) = \\emptyset $ whenever $ i \\neq j $.\n</tool_response>\nThe key issue identified in the consistency check is the off-by-one indexing error. The natural language statement uses 1-indexed notation (r₆ + r₇ + ... + rₙ), but Lean's `Fin n` is 0-indexed (values 0, 1, ..., n-1). Therefore:\n- r₆ (1-indexed) corresponds to index 5 (0-indexed)\n- rₙ (1-indexed) corresponds to index n-1 (0-indexed)\n\nThe condition `i.val ≥ 6` was incorrect; it should be `i.val ≥ 5` to capture indices 5, 6, ..., n-1 (0-indexed), which corresponds to r₆, r₇, ..., rₙ (1-indexed).\n\n```lean4\nimport Mathlib\n\nopen EuclideanSpace Finset\n\n/-- \nFor n ≥ 6, given n pairwise disjoint disks with decreasing radii r₁ ≥ r₂ ≥ ... ≥ rₙ,\nand points Pᵢ inside each disk Dᵢ, the sum of distances from any point A to all Pᵢ\nis at least the sum of radii r₆ + r₇ + ... + rₙ.\n-/\ntheorem disk_distance_inequality {n : ℕ} (hn : n ≥ 6)\n  (center : Fin n → EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin 2))\n  (radius : Fin n → ℝ)\n  (h_radius_nonneg : ∀ i, 0 ≤ radius i)\n  (h_radius_decreasing : ∀ i j : Fin n, i ≤ j → radius j ≤ radius i)\n  (h_disjoint : (Set.univ : Set (Fin n)).PairwiseDisjoint (fun i => Metric.closedBall (center i) (radius i)))\n  (P : Fin n → EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin 2))\n  (h_P_in_disk : ∀ i, P i ∈ Metric.closedBall (center i) (radius i))\n  (A : EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin 2)) :\n  ∑ i : Fin n, dist A (P i) ≥ ∑ i : Fin n, if i.val ≥ 5 then radius i else 0 := by sorry\n```\n\n[syntax_rewrite] unicode subscript digits → ASCII\n```lean4\nimport Mathlib\n\nopen EuclideanSpace Finset\n\n/-- \nFor n ≥ 6, given n pairwise disjoint disks with decreasing radii r1 ≥ r2 ≥ ... ≥ rₙ,\nand points Pᵢ inside each disk Dᵢ, the sum of distances from any point A to all Pᵢ\nis at least the sum of radii r6 + r7 + ... + rₙ.\n-/\ntheorem disk_distance_inequality {n : ℕ} (hn : n ≥ 6)\n  (center : Fin n → EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin 2))\n  (radius : Fin n → ℝ)\n  (h_radius_nonneg : ∀ i, 0 ≤ radius i)\n  (h_radius_decreasing : ∀ i j : Fin n, i ≤ j → radius j ≤ radius i)\n  (h_disjoint : (Set.univ : Set (Fin n)).PairwiseDisjoint (fun i => Metric.closedBall (center i) (radius i)))\n  (P : Fin n → EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin 2))\n  (h_P_in_disk : ∀ i, P i ∈ Metric.closedBall (center i) (radius i))\n  (A : EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin 2)) :\n  ∑ i : Fin n, dist A (P i) ≥ ∑ i : Fin n, if i.val ≥ 5 then radius i else 0 := by sorry\n```\n\n# Round 3 (Semantic Self-validation)\n\nYour brief analysis:\n1. Math Assertion Analysis: \n   - Variables: Integer $n \\geq 6$, $n$ disks $D_1, \\dots, D_n$ with radii $r_1 \\geq \\dots \\geq r_n$, points $P_i \\in D_i$, arbitrary point $A$.\n   - Constraints: Disks are pairwise disjoint.\n   - Conclusion: $\\sum_{i=1}^n AP_i \\geq \\sum_{j=6}^n r_j$. The RHS sum starts from the 6th radius ($r_6$) in 1-based indexing.\n\n2. Lean Statement Analysis (Proof Ignored):\n   - Variables: `n : ℕ` with `n ≥ 6`, centers and radii indexed by `Fin n` (0-based), points `P`, point `A`.\n   - Constraints: Radii non-negative, decreasing (`i ≤ j → radius j ≤ radius i`), pairwise disjoint closed balls.\n   - Conclusion: `∑ i : Fin n, dist A (P i) ≥ ∑ i : Fin n, if i.val ≥ 5 then radius i else 0`.\n   - The RHS sums `radius i` for indices `i` where `i.val ≥ 5`.\n\n3. Comparative Verification:\n   - **Indexing Correspondence**: The natural language uses 1-based indexing ($r_1, \\dots, r_n$), while Lean uses 0-based indexing (`Fin n` values $0, \\dots, n-1$).\n   - The 6th radius $r_6$ corresponds to Lean index `5` (since $0 \\to r_1, \\dots, 5 \\to r_6$).\n   - The Lean condition `i.val ≥ 5` correctly selects indices $5, 6, \\dots, n-1$, which correspond to radii $r_6, r_7, \\dots, r_n$.\n   - This matches the mathematical requirement $\\sum_{j=6}^n r_j$.\n   - All other constraints (disjointness, decreasing radii, point containment) are faithfully represented.\n   - The previous version (noted in comments) used `i.val ≥ 6`, which was incorrect; the current version uses `i.val ≥ 5`, which is correct.\n\n4. Conclusion: The Lean statement is a correct and exact formalization of the mathematical problem.\n\n5. Accuracy Confirmation: \n   - The indexing condition `i.val ≥ 5` correctly captures the sum from $r_6$ to $r_n$ under 0-based indexing.\n   - Variable domains, quantifiers, and logical structure align perfectly with the natural language statement.\n   - The fix from the previous iteration (changing `≥ 6` to `≥ 5`) resolves the off-by-one error.\n\nResult: Correct\n\nTherefore, the outcome of the consistency verification is: Correct\n\n</round>\n\n</think>\n\n<answer>\n\n```lean4\nimport Mathlib\n\nopen EuclideanSpace Finset\n\n/-- \nFor n ≥ 6, given n pairwise disjoint disks with decreasing radii r1 ≥ r2 ≥ ... ≥ rₙ,\nand points Pᵢ inside each disk Dᵢ, the sum of distances from any point A to all Pᵢ\nis at least the sum of radii r6 + r7 + ... + rₙ.\n-/\ntheorem disk_distance_inequality {n : ℕ} (hn : n ≥ 6)\n  (center : Fin n → EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin 2))\n  (radius : Fin n → ℝ)\n  (h_radius_nonneg : ∀ i, 0 ≤ radius i)\n  (h_radius_decreasing : ∀ i j : Fin n, i ≤ j → radius j ≤ radius i)\n  (h_disjoint : (Set.univ : Set (Fin n)).PairwiseDisjoint (fun i => Metric.closedBall (center i) (radius i)))\n  (P : Fin n → EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin 2))\n  (h_P_in_disk : ∀ i, P i ∈ Metric.closedBall (center i) (radius i))\n  (A : EuclideanSpace ℝ (Fin 2)) :\n  ∑ i : Fin n, dist A (P i) ≥ ∑ i : Fin n, if i.val ≥ 5 then radius i else 0 := by sorry\n```\n\n</answer>"}], "informal_statement": "Let $n \\geqslant 6$ be an integer. In the plane, we have arranged $n$ pairwise disjoint disks $D_{1}, D_{2}, \\ldots, D_{n}$ with radii $r_{1} \\geqslant r_{2} \\geqslant \\ldots \\geqslant r_{n}$. For every integer $i \\leqslant n$, consider a point $P_{i}$ inside the disk $D_{i}$. Finally, let $A$ be any point in the plane. Prove that\n\n$$\nA P_{1}+A P_{2}+\\ldots+A P_{n} \\geqslant r_{6}+r_{7}+\\ldots+r_{n}\n$$", "num_rounds": 3, "num_tool_calls": 6, "tools_used": ["leansearch"], "success": true, "syntax_pass": true, "syn