and prime $p$ with $2 < n$, $p \\leq n$, and $2n < 3p$, the exponent of $p$ in the prime factorization of the central binomial coefficient $\\binom{2n}{n}$ is zero.\n[6] Nat.Prime.emultiplicity_choose_prime_pow_add_emultiplicity  (theorem)\n    (hp : p.Prime) (hkn : k ≤ p ^ n) (hk0 : k ≠ 0) : emultiplicity p (choose (p ^ n) k) + emultiplicity p k = n\n    For a prime number $p$, natural numbers $k,n$ satisfying $0 < k \\leq p^n$, the sum of the exponent of $p$ in the binomial coefficient $\\binom{p^n}{k}$ and the exponent of $p$ in $k$ equals $n$. In symbols: $v_p\\left(\\binom{p^n}{k}\\right) + v_p(k) = n$, where $v_p(m)$ denotes the largest power of $p$ dividing $m$.\n[7] WittVector.map_frobeniusPoly.key₁  (theorem)\n    (n j : ℕ) (hj : j < p ^ n) : p ^ (n - v p (j + 1)) ∣ (p ^ n).choose (j + 1)\n    For natural numbers $ n $ and $ j $ with $ j < p^n $, the binomial coefficient $ \\binom{p^n}{j+1} $ is divisible by $ p $ raised to the power $ n - v_p(j+1) $, where $ v_p(j+1) $ denotes the $ p $-adic valuation of $ j+1 $. That is, $ p^{n - v_p(j+1)} $ divides $ \\binom{p^n}{j+1} $.\n[8] Nat.factorization_choose'  (theorem)\n    {p n k b : ℕ} (hp : p.Prime) (hnb : log p (n + k) < b) :\n  (choose (n + k) k).factorization p = #({i ∈ Ico 1 b | p ^ i ≤ k % p ^ i + n % p ^ i})\n    For a prime number $ p $, natural numbers $ n $, $ k $, and $ b $ such that the base-$ p $ logarithm of $ n + k $ is less than $ b $, the exponent of $ p $ in the prime factorization of the binomial coefficient $ \\binom{n + k}{k} $ is equal to the number of integers $ i $ in the interval $ [1, b) $ for which $ p^i $ divides the sum of $ n \\mod p^i $ and $ k \\mod p^i $.\n</tool_response>\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"leansearch\", \"arguments\": {\"query\": \"sum binomial coefficients floor 2p/3 prime squared divisibility\", \"num_results\": 8}}\n</tool_call>\n<tool_response>\n[1] Nat.factorization_centralBinom_of_two_mul_self_lt_three_mul  (theorem)\n    (n_big : 2 < n) (p_le_n : p ≤ n) (big : 2 * n < 3 * p) : (centralBinom n).factorization p = 0\n    For natural numbers $n$ and prime $p$ with $2 < n$, $p \\leq n$, and $2n < 3p$, the exponent of $p$ in the prime factorization of the central binomial coefficient $\\binom{2n}{n}$ is zero.\n[2] Nat.Prime.dvd_choose_add  (theorem)\n    (hp : Prime p) (hap : a < p) (hbp : b < p) (h : p ≤ a + b) : p ∣ choose (a + b) a\n    For any prime number $ p $ and natural numbers $ a, b $ such that $ a < p $, $ b < p $, and $ p \\leq a + b $, the prime $ p $ divides the binomial coefficient $ \\binom{a + b}{a} $.\n[3] Nat.factorization_choose_le_one  (theorem)\n    (p_large : n < p ^ 2) : (choose n k).factorization p ≤ 1\n    For a prime $ p $ and natural numbers $ n, k $ such that $ n < p^2 $, the exponent of $ p $ in the prime factorization of the binomial coefficient $ \\binom{n}{k} $ satisfies $ v_p\\left(\\binom{n}{k}\\right) \\leq 1 $.\n[4] Choose.choose_modEq_choose_mod_mul_choose_div  (theorem)\n    : choose n k ≡ choose (n % p) (k % p) * choose (n / p) (k / p) [ZMOD p]\n    For a prime number $ p $ and natural numbers $ n, k $, the binomial coefficient $ \\binom{n}{k} $ satisfies the congruence $ \\binom{n}{k} \\equiv \\binom{n \\bmod p}{k \\bmod p} \\cdot \\binom{\\lfloor n/p \\rfloor}{\\lfloor k/p \\rfloor} \\pmod{p} $, where $ \\lfloor x \\rfloor $ denotes integer division (floor function).\n[5] Nat.multiplicity_choose_aux  (theorem)\n    {p n b k : ℕ} (hp : p.Prime) (hkn : k ≤ n) :\n  ∑ i ∈ Finset.Ico 1 b, n / p ^ i =\n    ((∑ i ∈ Finset.Ico 1 b, k / p ^ i) + ∑ i ∈ Finset.Ico 1 b, (n - k) / p ^ i) +\n      #({i ∈ Ico 1 b | p ^ i ≤ k % p ^ i + (n - k) % p ^ i})\n    For a prime $ p $, natural numbers $ n, k, b $ with $ k \\leq n $, the sum $ \\sum_{i=1}^b \\left\\lfloor \\frac{n}{p^i} \\right\\rfloor $ equals $ \\left( \\sum_{i=1}^b \\left\\lfloor \\frac{k}{p^i} \\right\\rfloor + \\sum_{i=1}^b \\left\\lfloor \\frac{n - k}{p^i} \\right\\rfloor \\right) $ plus the cardinality of the set $ \\{ i \\in [1, b] \\mid p^i \\mid (k \\bmod p^i + (n - k) \\bmod p^i) \\} $.\n[6] Nat.factorization_choose_of_lt_three_mul  (theorem)\n    (hp' : p ≠ 2) (hk : p ≤ k) (hk' : p ≤ n - k) (hn : n < 3 * p) : (choose n k).factorization p = 0\n    For a prime $ p \\neq 2 $ and natural numbers $ k, n $ satisfying $ p \\leq k $, $ p \\leq n - k $, and $ n < 3p $, the exponent of $ p $ in the prime factorization of the binomial coefficient $ \\binom{n}{k} $ is zero, i.e., $ p $ does not divide $ \\binom{n}{k} $.\n[7] centralBinom_factorization_small  (theorem)\n    (n : ℕ) (n_large : 2 < n) (no_prime : ∀ p : ℕ, p.Prime → n < p → 2 * n < p) :\n  centralBinom n = ∏ p ∈ Finset.range (2 * n / 3 + 1), p ^ (centralBinom n).factorization p\n    For any natural number $n > 2$, if no prime $p > n$ divides $2n$, then the central binomial coefficient $\\binom{2n}{n}$ equals the product over primes $p$ in the range $\\left[0, \\frac{2n}{3} + 1\\right]$ of $p$ raised to the exponent of $p$ in its prime factorization.\n[8] Nat.Prime.dvd_choose_pow  (theorem)\n    (hp : Prime p) (hk : k ≠ 0) (hkp : k ≠ p ^ n) : p ∣ (p ^ n).choose k\n    For a prime number $ p $, positive integers $ n $ and $ k $ with $ 0 < k < p^n $, the prime $ p $ divides the binomial coefficient $\\binom{p^n}{k}$.\n</tool_response>\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"leansearch\", \"arguments\": {\"query\": \"floor function Nat.div prime 2p/3\", \"num_results\": 8}}\n</tool_call>\n<tool_response>\n[1] Nat.floorDiv_eq_div  (theorem)\n    (a b : ℕ) : a ⌊/⌋ b = a / b\n    For any natural numbers $a$ and $b$, the floor division of $a$ by $b$ is equal to the integer division of $a$ by $b$, i.e., $a \\, ext{⌊/⌋}\\, b = a / b$.\n[2] Rat.natFloor_natCast_div_natCast  (theorem)\n    (n d : ℕ) : ⌊(↑n / ↑d : ℚ)⌋₊ = n / d\n    For any natural numbers $n$ and $d$, the floor of the rational number $n/d$ is equal to the integer division of $n$ by $d$, i.e., $\\left\\lfloor \\frac{n}{d} \\right\\rfloor = \\left\\lfloor \\frac{n}{d} \\right\\rfloor_{\\mathbb{N}}$.\n[3