{"messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "Think step by step to translate the mathematical problem in natural language to Lean 4, and verify the consistency.\nProve that if $|G|=2907$ then $G$ is not simple."}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "<think>\n# Round 1 (Autoformalization)\n\n<round>\n\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"leansearch\", \"arguments\": {\"query\": \"simple group order finite group\", \"num_results\": 8}}\n</tool_call>\n<tool_response>\n[1] IsSimpleGroup.finite  (theorem)\n    : Finite α\n    The group $\\alpha$ is finite.\n[2] IsSimpleAddGroup.finite  (theorem)\n    ∀ {α : Type u_1} [inst : AddCommGroup α] [IsSimpleAddGroup α], Finite α\n    Every additive commutative group that is simple (i.e., has no nontrivial subgroups) is a finite group.\n[3] IsSimpleAddGroup.prime_card  (theorem)\n    ∀ {α : Type u_1} [inst : AddCommGroup α] [IsSimpleAddGroup α], Nat.Prime (Nat.card α)\n    If $ G $ is a finite additive commutative group and $ G $ is simple (i.e., it has no nontrivial subgroups), then the cardinality of $ G $ is a prime number.\n[4] isOfFinOrder_of_finite  (theorem)\n    (x : G) : IsOfFinOrder x\n    In a finite group $ G $, every element $ x \\in G $ has finite order, meaning there exists a positive integer $ n $ such that $ x^n = 1 $, where $ 1 $ denotes the identity element of $ G $.\n[5] isSimpleGroup_of_prime_card  (theorem)\n    {p : ℕ} [hp : Fact p.Prime] (h : Nat.card α = p) : IsSimpleGroup α\n    If a group $\\alpha$ has cardinality equal to a prime number $p$, then $\\alpha$ is a simple group.\n[6] exists_zpow_eq_one  (theorem)\n    (x : G) : ∃ (i : ℤ) (_ : i ≠ 0), x ^ (i : ℤ) = 1\n    In a finite group $ G $, for every element $ x \\in G $, there exists a non-zero integer $ i $ such that $ x^i = 1 $, where $ 1 $ denotes the identity element of the group.\n[7] Group.is_simple_iff_prime_card  (theorem)\n    [Group α] [IsMulCommutative α] : IsSimpleGroup α ↔ (Nat.card α).Prime\n    For any abelian group $\\alpha$, $\\alpha$ is simple if and only if the cardinality of $\\alpha$ is a prime number.\n[8] CommGroup.is_simple_iff_isCyclic_and_prime_card  (theorem)\n    ∀ {α : Type u_1} [inst : CommGroup α], IsSimpleGroup α ↔ Nat.Prime (Nat.card α)\n    For any commutative group $ G $, $ G $ is simple if and only if the cardinality of $ G $ is a prime number.\n</tool_response>\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"leansearch\", \"arguments\": {\"query\": \"group order not simple composite\", \"num_results\": 8}}\n</tool_call>\n<tool_response>\n[1] IsSimpleAddGroup.prime_card  (theorem)\n    ∀ {α : Type u_1} [inst : AddCommGroup α] [IsSimpleAddGroup α], Nat.Prime (Nat.card α)\n    If $ G $ is a finite additive commutative group and $ G $ is simple (i.e., it has no nontrivial subgroups), then the cardinality of $ G $ is a prime number.\n[2] CommGroup.is_simple_iff_isCyclic_and_prime_card  (theorem)\n    ∀ {α : Type u_1} [inst : CommGroup α], IsSimpleGroup α ↔ Nat.Prime (Nat.card α)\n    For any commutative group $ G $, $ G $ is simple if and only if the cardinality of $ G $ is a prime number.\n[3] isSimpleGroup_of_prime_card  (theorem)\n    {p : ℕ} [hp : Fact p.Prime] (h : Nat.card α = p) : IsSimpleGroup α\n    If a group $\\alpha$ has cardinality equal to a prime number $p$, then $\\alpha$ is a simple group.\n[4] CommGroup.is_simple_iff_prime_card  (theorem)\n    [CommGroup α] : IsSimpleGroup α ↔ (Nat.card α).Prime\n    A commutative group $G$ is simple if and only if the cardinality of $G$ is a prime number.\n[5] Group.is_simple_iff_prime_card  (theorem)\n    [Group α] [IsMulCommutative α] : IsSimpleGroup α ↔ (Nat.card α).Prime\n    For any abelian group $\\alpha$, $\\alpha$ is simple if and only if the cardinality of $\\alpha$ is a prime number.\n[6] AddCommGroup.is_simple_iff_prime_card  (theorem)\n    ∀ {α : Type u_1} [inst : AddCommGroup α], IsSimpleAddGroup α ↔ Nat.Prime (Nat.card α)\n    An additive commutative group $ \\alpha $ is simple (has no nontrivial subgroups) if and only if the cardinality of $ \\alpha $ is a prime number.\n[7] AddGroup.is_simple_iff_prime_card  (theorem)\n    ∀ {α : Type u_1} [inst : AddGroup α] [IsAddCommutative α], IsSimpleAddGroup α ↔ Nat.Prime (Nat.card α)\n    For any additive commutative group $\\alpha$, the group is simple (has no nontrivial subgroups) if and only if the cardinality of $\\alpha$ is a prime number.\n[8] IsSimpleGroup.prime_card  (theorem)\n    : (Nat.card α).Prime\n    The order of a simple cyclic group is a prime number, i.e., if $\\alpha$ is a simple cyclic group, then its cardinality $|\\alpha|$ is a prime number.\n</tool_response>\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"leansearch\", \"arguments\": {\"query\": \"IsSimpleGroup card order theorem\", \"num_results\": 8}}\n</tool_call>\n<tool_response>\n[1] isSimpleGroup_of_prime_card  (theorem)\n    {p : ℕ} [hp : Fact p.Prime] (h : Nat.card α = p) : IsSimpleGroup α\n    If a group $\\alpha$ has cardinality equal to a prime number $p$, then $\\alpha$ is a simple group.\n[2] IsSimpleGroup.prime_card  (theorem)\n    : (Nat.card α).Prime\n    The order of a simple cyclic group is a prime number, i.e., if $\\alpha$ is a simple cyclic group, then its cardinality $|\\alpha|$ is a prime number.\n[3] CommGroup.is_simple_iff_isCyclic_and_prime_card  (theorem)\n    ∀ {α : Type u_1} [inst : CommGroup α], IsSimpleGroup α ↔ Nat.Prime (Nat.card α)\n    For any commutative group $ G $, $ G $ is simple if and only if the cardinality of $ G $ is a prime number.\n[4] CommGroup.is_simple_iff_prime_card  (theorem)\n    [CommGroup α] : IsSimpleGroup α ↔ (Nat.card α).Prime\n    A commutative group $G$ is simple if and only if the cardinality of $G$ is a prime number.\n[5] Group.is_simple_iff_prime_card  (theorem)\n    [Group α] [IsMulCommutative α] : IsSimpleGroup α ↔ (Nat.card α).Prime\n    For any abelian group $\\alpha$, $\\alpha$ is simple if and only if the cardinality of $\\alpha$ is a prime number.\n[6] IsSimpleAddGroup.prime_card  (theorem)\n    ∀ {α : Type u_1} [inst : AddCommGroup α] [IsSimpleAddGroup α], Nat.Prime (Nat.card α)\n    If $ G $ is a finite additive commutative group and $ G $ is simple (i.e., it has no nontrivial subgroups), then the cardinality of $ G $ is a prime number.\n[7] isSimpleAddGroup_of_prime_card  (theorem)\n    ∀ {α : Type u_1} [inst : AddGroup α] {p : ℕ} [hp : Fact (Nat.Prime p)], Nat.card α = p → IsSimpleAddGroup α\n    If the cardinality of an additive group $\\alpha$ is a prime number $p$, then $\\alpha$ is a simple additive group, meaning it contains no nontrivial subgroups.\n[8] AddGroup.is_simple_iff_prime_card  (theorem)\n    ∀ {α : Type u_1} [inst : AddGroup α] [IsAddCommutative α], IsSimpleAddGroup α ↔ Nat.Prime (Nat.card α)\n    For any additive commutative group $\\alpha$, the group is simple (has no nontrivial subgroups) if and only if the cardinality of $\\alpha$ is a prime number.\n</tool_response>\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"leansearch\", \"arguments\": {\"query\": \"IsSimpleGroup definition simple group no normal subgroup\", \"num_results\": 8}}\n\n# Round 1 (Semantic Self-validation)\n\nLean compiler reported errors: unexpected token '('; expected command\n\nTherefore, the outcome of the consistency verification is: Incorrect\n\n</round>\n\n# Round 2 (Autoformalization)\n\n<round>\n\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"leansearch\", \"arguments\": {\"query\": \"simple group definition order cardFintype\", \"num_results\": 8}}\n</tool_call>\n<tool_response>\n[1] IsSimpleGroup  (inductive)\n    (G : Type u_1) → [Group G] → Prop\n    A group $G$ is simple if its only normal subgroups are the trivial subgroup and $G$ itself.\n[2] IsSimpleGroup.mk  (constructor)\n    ∀ {G : Type u_1} [inst : Group G] [toNontrivial : Nontrivial G],\n  (∀ (H : Subgroup G), H.Normal → H = ⊥ ∨ H = ⊤) → IsSimpleGroup G\n    If a non-trivial group $ G $ has the property that every normal subgroup is either the trivial subgroup or $ G $ itself, then $ G $ is a simple group.\n[3] isSimpleGroup_iff  (theorem)\n    ∀ (G : Type u_1) [inst : Group G], IsSimpleGroup G ↔ Nontrivial G ∧ ∀ (H : Subgroup G), H.Normal → H = ⊥ ∨ H = ⊤\n    A group $G$ is simple if and only if it is non-trivial and every normal subgroup of $G$ is either the trivial subgroup $\\{e\\}$ or the entire group $G$ itself.\n[4] CommGroup.is_simple_iff_prime_card  (theorem)\n    [CommGroup α] : IsSimpleGroup α ↔ (Nat.card α).Prime\n    A commutative group $G$ is simple if and only if the cardinality of $G$ is a prime number.\n[5] Fintype.IsSimpleOrder.card  (theorem)\n    : Fintype.card α = 2\n    For a simple order $\\alpha$ (where every element is either the least element $\\bot$ or the greatest element $\\top$), the cardinality of $\\alpha$ is 2, i.e., $\\#\\alpha = 2$.\n[6] isSimpleGroup_of_prime_card  (theorem)\n    {p : ℕ} [hp : Fact p.Prime] (h : Nat.card α = p) : IsSimpleGroup α\n    If a group $\\alpha$ has cardinality equal to a prime number $p$, then $\\alpha$ is a simple group.\n[7] Group.is_simple_iff_prime_card  (theorem)\n    [Group α] [IsMulCommutative α] : IsSimpleGroup α ↔ (Nat.card α).Prime\n    For any abelian group $\\alpha$, $\\alpha$ is simple if and only if the cardinality of $\\alpha$ is a prime number.\n[8] CommGroup.is_simple_iff_isCyclic_and_prime_card  (theorem)\n    ∀ {α : Type u_1} [inst : CommGroup α], IsSimpleGroup α ↔ Nat.Prime (Nat.card α)\n    For any commutative group $ G $, $ G $ is simple if and only if the cardinality of $ G $ is a prime number.\n</tool_response>\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"leansearch\", \"arguments\": {\"query\": \"Fintype.card group order Nat.card\", \"num_results\": 8}}\n</tool_call>\n<tool_response>\n[1] orderOf_eq_card_of_zpowers_eq_top  (theorem)\n    {g : G} (h : Subgroup.zpowers g = ⊤) : orderOf g = Nat.card G\n    If the cyclic subgroup generated by an element $ g $ in a group $ G $ is equal to the entire group $ G $, then the order of $ g $ (the smallest positive integer $ n $ such that $ g^n = 1 $) is equal to the cardinality of $ G $.\n[2] Fintype.card_eq_nat_card  (theorem)\n    {_ : Fintype α} : Fintype.card α = Nat.card α\n    For a finite type $\\alpha$, the cardinality $\\# \\alpha$ is equal to the natural number cardinality of $\\alpha$, i.e., $\\# \\alpha = \\text{Nat.card}(\\alpha)$.\n[3] orderOf_le_card  (theorem)\n    [Finite G] : orderOf x ≤ Nat.card G\n    In a finite group $ G $, the order of any element $ x $ is less than or equal to the cardinality of $ G $. The order of an element is defined as the smallest positive integer $ n $ such that $ x^n = 1 $, where $ 1 $ is the identity element, and the cardinality $ |G| $ denotes the number of elements in the finite group.\n[4] orderOf_eq_card_of_forall_mem_zpowers  (theorem)\n    {g : α} (hx : ∀ x, x ∈ zpowers g) : orderOf g = Nat.card α\n    For a group element $ g $, if every element of the group is contained in the cyclic subgroup generated by $ g $, then the order of $ g $ is equal to the cardinality of the group.\n[5] orderOf_eq_card_of_forall_mem_powers  (theorem)\n    {g : α} (hx : ∀ x, x ∈ Submonoid.powers g) : orderOf g = Nat.card α\n    If every element of a group $\\alpha$ is contained in the submonoid generated by the powers of an element $g$, then the order of $g$ is equal to the cardinality of $\\alpha$, i.e., $\\operatorname{orderOf}(g) = \\operatorname{Nat.card}(\\alpha)$.\n[6] orderOf_dvd_natCard  (theorem)\n    {G : Type*} [Group G] (x : G) : orderOf x ∣ Nat.card G\n    For any element $x$ in a group $G$, the order of $x$ divides the cardinality of $G$, i.e., $\\operatorname{orderOf}(x) \\mid \\operatorname{Nat.card}(G)$.\n[7] Nat.card_zpowers  (theorem)\n    : Nat.card (zpowers a) = orderOf a\n    The cardinality of the cyclic subgroup generated by an element $ a $ in a group is equal to the order of $ a $, i.e., $ \\sharp\\langle a \\rangle = \\operatorname{orderOf}(a) $, where $ \\langle a \\rangle $ denotes the subgroup of integer powers $ \\{a^k \\mid k \\in \\mathbb{Z}\\} $.\n[8] pow_card_eq_one'  (theorem)\n    {G : Type*} [Group G] {x : G} : x ^ Nat.card G = 1\n    For any group $G$ and any element $x \\in G$, it holds that $x^{\\text{card}(G)} = 1$, where $\\text{card}(G)$ denotes the cardinality of $G$ as a natural number (with infinite cardinals mapped to zero).\n</tool_response>\nNow I have enough information. The problem asks to prove that a group of order 2907 is not simple. In Lean 4, I need to:\n1. Use `IsSimpleGroup` for the simple group property\n2. Use `Fintype` and `Fintype.card` for finite groups with specific cardinality\n3. State that if the cardinality is 2907, then the group is NOT simple\n\nLet me factor 2907 to understand the number: 2907 = 3 × 969 = 3 × 3 × 323 = 9 × 323 = 9 × 17 × 19\n\nSo 2907 = 3² × 17 × 19, which is not prime. The theorem should state that a group of order 2907 cannot be simple.\n\n```lean4\nimport Mathlib\n\ntheorem not_simple_of_card_2907 {G : Type*} [Group G] [Fintype G] \n  (h : Fintype.card G = 2907) : ¬ IsSimpleGroup G := by sorry\n```\n\n# Round 2 (Semantic Self-validation)\n\nYour brief analysis:\n1. Math Assertion Analysis: The problem asks to prove that any group $G$ with order (cardinality) 2907 is not a simple group. Key components: $G$ is a group, $|G| = 2907$ (finite), conclusion is $\\neg$ (G is simple).\n2. Lean Statement Analysis (Proof Ignored): The theorem declares a variable `G` of type `Type*` with `Group` and `Fintype` instances. The hypothesis `h` asserts `Fintype.card G = 2907`. The conclusion is `¬ IsSimpleGroup G`.\n3. Comparative Verification: \n   - \"Group $G$\" matches `[Group G]`.\n   - \"$|G|=2907$\" matches `Fintype.card G = 2907` (assuming finite, which is explicit in Lean).\n   - \"$G$ is not simple\" matches `¬ IsSimpleGroup G`.\n   - The logical structure (implication) is preserved.\n   - `IsSimpleGroup` in Mathlib4 correctly encodes the definition of a simple group (non-trivial and no proper normal subgroups). Since 2907 > 1, the non-triviality condition is satisfied, making the negation equivalent to the existence of a proper normal subgroup.\n4. Conclusion: The Lean statement is a precise and faithful formalization of the natural language statement.\n5. Accuracy Confirmation: All elements match. The variable types, constraints, and logical conclusion correspond exactly between the mathematical problem and the Lean code.\n\nResult: Correct\n\nTherefore, the outcome of the consistency verification is: Correct\n\n</round>\n\n</think>\n\n<answer>\n\n```lean4\nimport Mathlib\n\ntheorem not_simple_of_card_2907 {G : Type*} [Group G] [Fintype G] \n  (h : Fintype.card G = 2907) : ¬ IsSimpleGroup G := by sorry\n```\n\n</answer>"}], "informal_statement": "Prove that if $|G|=2907$ then $G$ is not simple.", "num_rounds": 2, "num_tool_calls": 5, "tools_used": ["leansearch"], "success": true, "syntax_pass": true, "syntax_rewrites": [], "retrieval_domain": "math", "dataset_name": "proofnet_test", "question_id": "125"}
{"messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "Think step by step to translate the mathematical problem in natural language to Lean 4, and verify the consistency.\nXXIII OM - I - Problem 4\n\nPoints $ A $ and $ B $ do not belong to the plane $ \\pi $. Find the set of all points $ M \\in \\pi $ with the property that the lines $ AM $ and $ BM $ form equal angles with the plane $ \\pi $."}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "<think>\n# Round 1 (Autoformalization)\n\n<round>\n\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"leansearch\", \"arguments\": {\"query\": \"angle between line and plane 3D geometry\", \"num_results\": 8}}\n</tool_call>\n<tool_response>\n[1] EuclideanGeometry.angle  (definition)\n    (p₁ p₂ p₃ : P) : ℝ\n    The angle at point $ p_2 $ formed by the points $ p_1 $, $ p_2 $, and $ p_3 $ in an affine space is defined as $ \\arccos\\left( \\frac{\\langle p_1 - p_2, p_3 - p_2 \\rangle}{\\|p_1 - p_2\\| \\cdot \\|p_3 - p_2\\|} \\right) $, where $ \\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle $ denotes the inner product and $ \\|\\cdot\\| $ denotes the norm induced by the inner product.\n[2] InnerProductGeometry.angle  (definition)\n    (x y : V) : ℝ\n    The angle between vectors $ x $ and $ y $ in an inner product space is defined as $ \\arccos\\left( \\frac{\\langle x, y \\rangle}{\\|x\\| \\cdot \\|y\\|} \\right) $, where $ \\langle x, y \\rangle $ denotes the inner product and $ \\|x\\| $, $ \\|y\\| $ denote the norms of $ x $ and $ y $, respectively.\n[3] EuclideanGeometry.dist_orthogonalProjection_eq_iff_angle_eq  (theorem)\n    {p p' : P} {s₁ s₂ : AffineSubspace ℝ P} [s₁.direction.HasOrthogonalProjection] [s₂.direction.HasOrthogonalProjection]\n  (hp' : p' ∈ s₁ ⊓ s₂) :\n  haveI : Nonempty s₁ := ⟨p', hp'.1⟩\n  haveI : Nonempty s₂ := ⟨p', hp'.2⟩\n  dist p (orthogonalProjection s₁ p) = dist p (orthogonalProjection s₂ p) ↔\n    ∠ p p' (orthogonalProjection s₁ p) = ∠ p p' (orthogonalProjection s₂ p)\n    For points $ p $ and $ p' $ in an affine space, and affine subspaces $ s_1 $, $ s_2 $ with orthogonal projections, if $ p' $ lies in the intersection $ s_1 \\cap s_2 $, then the distance from $ p $ to the orthogonal projection onto $ s_1 $ equals the distance to the orthogonal projection onto $ s_2 $ if and only if the angles $ \\angle p p' (\\operatorname{proj}_{s_1} p) $ and $ \\angle p p' (\\operatorname{proj}_{s_2} p) $ are equal. The angle is defined via $ \\arccos\\left(\\frac{\\langle v, w \\rangle}{\\|v\\| \\cdot \\|w\\|}\\right) $ for vectors $ v, w $.\n[4] Wbtw.angle₂₁₃_eq_zero_of_ne  (theorem)\n    {p₁ p₂ p₃ : P} (h : Wbtw ℝ p₁ p₂ p₃) (hp₂p₁ : p₂ ≠ p₁) : ∠ p₂ p₁ p₃ = 0\n    If $ p_2 $ lies between $ p_1 $ and $ p_3 $ in an affine space over $ \\mathbb{R} $ and $ p_2 \\neq p_1 $, then the angle at $ p_1 $ formed by the points $ p_2, p_1, p_3 $ is zero, i.e., $ \\angle p_2 p_1 p_3 = 0 $.\n[5] EuclideanGeometry.angle_self_orthogonalProjection  (theorem)\n    (p : P) {p' : P} {s : AffineSubspace ℝ P} [s.direction.HasOrthogonalProjection] (h : p' ∈ s) :\n  haveI : Nonempty s := ⟨p', h⟩\n  ∠ p (orthogonalProjection s p) p' = π / 2\n    Given an affine space over the real numbers, for any point $ p $ and its orthogonal projection onto a non-empty affine subspace $ s $, if $ p' $ is a point in $ s $, then the angle formed at the projection between $ p $ and $ p' $ is a right angle, i.e., $ \\angle p (\\text{proj}_s(p)) p' = \\frac{\\pi}{2} $.\n[6] Wbtw.angle₂₃₁_eq_zero_of_ne  (theorem)\n    {p₁ p₂ p₃ : P} (h : Wbtw ℝ p₁ p₂ p₃) (hp₂p₃ : p₂ ≠ p₃) : ∠ p₂ p₃ p₁ = 0\n    If point $ p_2 $ lies between $ p_1 $ and $ p_3 $ in an affine space and $ p_2 eq p_3 $, then the angle at $ p_3 $ between $ p_2 $ and $ p_1 $ is zero, i.e., $ \\angle p_2 p_3 p_1 = 0 $.\n[7] EuclideanGeometry.oangle_orthogonalProjection_self  (theorem)\n    (p : P) {p' : P} {s : AffineSubspace ℝ P} [s.direction.HasOrthogonalProjection] (hp : p ∉ s) (h : p' ∈ s)\n  (hp' :\n    haveI : Nonempty s := ⟨p', h⟩;\n    p' ≠ orthogonalProjection s p) :\n  haveI : Nonempty s := ⟨p', h⟩\n  ∡ p' (orthogonalProjection s p) p = (π / 2 : ℝ) ∨ ∡ p' (orthogonalProjection s p) p = (-π / 2 : ℝ)\n    Given a point $ p $ not in an affine subspace $ s $, a point $ p' $ in $ s $, and the orthogonal projection $ q $ of $ p $ onto $ s $, if $ p' \\ne q $, then the oriented angle $ \\angle p' q p $ is either $ \\frac{\\pi}{2} $ or $ -\\frac{\\pi}{2} $. This characterizes the orthogonality between the vector $ p' - q $ and the vector $ p - q $ in the affine space.\n[8] Sbtw.angle₃₁₂_eq_zero  (theorem)\n    {p₁ p₂ p₃ : P} (h : Sbtw ℝ p₁ p₂ p₃) : ∠ p₃ p₁ p₂ = 0\n    If point $ p_2 $ lies strictly between points $ p_1 $ and $ p_3 $ in an affine space, then the angle at $ p_1 $ formed by points $ p_3 $, $ p_1 $, and $ p_2 $ is equal to zero, i.e., $ \\angle p_3 p_1 p_2 = 0 $.\n</tool_response>\nLooking at this problem, I need to formalize the condition for when lines AM and BM form equal angles with plane π.\n\nThe angle between a line and a plane is the angle between the line and its orthogonal projection onto the plane. For point M on plane π:\n- The orthogonal projection of A onto π is A'\n- The angle between line AM and plane π equals the angle at M between vectors MA and MA'\n- Similarly for B and B'\n\nLet me search for the relevant angle definitions in Mathlib to ensure correct formalization.\n\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"leansearch\", \"arguments\": {\"query\": \"angle between three points EuclideanGeometry angle definition\", \"num_results\": 5}}\n</tool_call>\n<tool_response>\n[1] EuclideanGeometry.angle  (definition)\n    (p₁ p₂ p₃ : P) : ℝ\n    The angle at point $ p_2 $ formed by the points $ p_1 $, $ p_2 $, and $ p_3 $ in an affine space is defined as $ \\arccos\\left( \\frac{\\langle p_1 - p_2, p_3 - p_2 \\rangle}{\\|p_1 - p_2\\| \\cdot \\|p_3 - p_2\\|} \\right) $, where $ \\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\rangle $ denotes the inner product and $ \\|\\cdot\\| $ denotes the norm induced by the inner product.\n[2] EuclideanGeometry.oangle  (definition)\n    (p₁ p₂ p₃ : P) : Real.Angle\n    The oriented angle at point $p_2$ formed by the vectors $\\vec{p_2p_1}$ and $\\vec{p_2p_3}$ in an affine space, computed using the orientation structure $o$. This returns an angle in $\\mathbb{R}/(2\\pi\\mathbb{Z})$ representing the signed angle between the vectors.\n[3] EuclideanGeometry.angle_le_pi  (theorem)\n    (p₁ p₂ p₃ : P) : ∠ p₁ p₂ p₃ ≤ π\n    In an affine space equipped with an inner product, the unoriented angle formed at point $ p_2 $ by points $ p_1 $, $ p_2 $, and $ p_3 $ satisfies $ \\angle p_1 p_2 p_3 \\leq \\pi $. This follows from the definition of the angle as $ \\arccos\\left( \\frac{\\langle p_1 - p_2, p_3 - p_2 \\rangle}{\\|p_1 - p_2\\| \\cdot \\|p_3 - p_2\\|} \\right) $, where the arccosine function outputs values in the interval $[0, \\pi]$.\"\n[4] EuclideanGeometry.term∠  (definition)\n    : Lean.ParserDescr✝\n    The parser for the angle symbol $\\angle$ in Euclidean geometry, used to denote unoriented affine angles between geometric objects.\n[5] EuclideanGeometry.angle_comm  (theorem)\n    (p₁ p₂ p₃ : P) : ∠ p₁ p₂ p₃ = ∠ p₃ p₂ p₁\n    For any three points $p_1, p_2, p_3$ in an affine space, the angle at $p_2$ formed by $p_1-p_2-p_3$ is equal to the angle at $p_2$ formed by $p_3-p_2-p_1$, i.e., $\\angle p_1 p_2 p_3 = \\angle p_3 p_2 p_1$.\n</tool_response>\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"leansearch\", \"arguments\": {\"query\": \"orthogonal projection AffineSubspace plane\", \"num_results\": 5}}\n</tool_call>\n<tool_response>\n[1] EuclideanGeometry.orthogonalProjection_apply'  (theorem)\n    (s : AffineSubspace 𝕜 P) [Nonempty s] [s.direction.HasOrthogonalProjection] {p} :\n  (orthogonalProjection s p : P) =\n    (s.direction.orthogonalProjection (p -ᵥ Classical.arbitrary s) : V) +ᵥ (Classical.arbitrary s : P)\n    For any non-empty affine subspace $ s $ over a real closed-like field $ \\mathbb{K} $ admitting an orthogonal projection, and for any point $ p $ in the ambient space $ P $, the orthogonal projection of $ p $ onto $ 