rem)\n    {s : AffineSubspace 𝕜 P} [Nonempty s] [s.direction.HasOrthogonalProjection] (p : P) : ↑(orthogonalProjection s p) ∈ s\n    For any non-empty affine subspace $ s $ over a real closed-like field $ \\mathbb{K} $, if the direction of $ s $ admits an orthogonal projection, then for every point $ p $ in the ambient space $ P $, the orthogonal projection of $ p $ onto $ s $ is an element of $ s $.\n[5] EuclideanGeometry.orthogonalProjection_apply_mem  (theorem)\n    (s : AffineSubspace 𝕜 P) [Nonempty s] [s.direction.HasOrthogonalProjection] {p x} (hx : x ∈ s) :\n  orthogonalProjection s p = (s.direction.orthogonalProjection (p -ᵥ x) : V) +ᵥ x\n    Given a non-empty affine subspace $ s $ over a real closed-like field $ \\mathbb{K} $ with an orthogonal projection on its direction, and a point $ x \\in s $, the orthogonal projection of any point $ p $ onto $ s $ is equal to the orthogonal projection of the vector difference $ p - x $ onto the direction subspace of $ s $, translated back by adding $ x $. Formally, $ \\text{proj}_s(p) = \\text{proj}_{s.\\text{direction}}(p - x) + x $.\n</tool_response>\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"leansearch\", \"arguments\": {\"query\": \"locus set of points equal angles plane\", \"num_results\": 5}}\n\n# Round 1 (Semantic Self-validation)\n\nLean compiler reported errors: unexpected token '('; expected command\n\nTherefore, the outcome of the consistency verification is: Incorrect\n\n</round>\n\n# Round 2 (Autoformalization)\n\n<round>\n\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"leansearch\", \"arguments\": {\"query\": \"angle between line and plane Euclidean space\", \"num_results\": 8}}\n</tool_call>\n<tool_response>\n[1] EuclideanGeometry.angle  (definition)\n    (p₁ p₂ p₃ : P) : ℝ\n    The angle at point $ p_2 $ formed by the points $ p_1 $, $ p_2 $, and $ p_3 $ in an affine space is defined as $ \\arccos\\left( \\frac{\\langle p_1 - p_2, p_3 - p_2 \\rangle}{\\|p_1 - p_2\\| \\cdot \\|p_3 - p_2\\|} \\right) $, where $ \\langle \\cdot, \\cdot \\ra