= s.direction.orthogonalProjection (p -ᵥ Classical.arbitrary s) +ᵥ Classical.arbitrary s\n    For a non-empty affine subspace $ s $ over a real closed-like field $ \\mathbb{K} $ with an orthogonal projection on its direction subspace, the orthogonal projection of any point $ p $ onto $ s $ is given by $ \\text{proj}_s(p) = \\text{proj}_{s.\\text{direction}}(p - x_0) + x_0 $, where $ x_0 $ is an arbitrarily chosen point in $ s $. This expresses the affine projection as the composition of translating $ p $ by $ -x_0 $, projecting orthogonally onto the direction subspace, and translating back by $ x_0 $.\n[7] EuclideanGeometry.orthogonalProjection_apply'  (theorem)\n    (s : AffineSubspace 𝕜 P) [Nonempty s] [s.direction.HasOrthogonalProjection] {p} :\n  (orthogonalProjection s p : P) =\n    (s.direction.orthogonalProjection (p -ᵥ Classical.arbitrary s) : V) +ᵥ (Classical.arbitrary s : P)\n    For any non-empty affine subspace $ s $ over a real closed-like field $ \\mathbb{K} $ admitting an orthogonal projection, and for any point $ p $ in the ambient space $ P $, the orthogonal projection of $ p $ onto $ s $ is given by $ \\text{proj}_s(p) = \\text{proj}_{\\text{dir}(s)}(p - x) + x $, where $ x $ is an arbitrarily chosen point in $ s $, $ \\text{dir}(s) $ denotes the direction subspace of $ s $, and $ \\text{proj}_{\\text{dir}(s)} $ is the orthogonal projection onto $ \\text{dir}(s) $. This formula expresses the projection as the combination of projecting the vector from $ x $ to $ p $ ont...\n[8] EuclideanGeometry.orthogonalProjection_vadd_eq_self  (theorem)\n    {s : AffineSubspace 𝕜 P} [Nonempty s] [s.direction.HasOrthogonalProjection] {p : P} (hp : p ∈ s) {v : V}\n  (hv : v ∈ s.directionᗮ) : orthogonalProjection s (v +ᵥ p) = ⟨p, hp⟩\n    For a non-empty affine subspace $s$ over a real closed-like field $\\mathbb{K}$ with an orthogonal projection, if $p$ is a point in $s$ and $v$ is a vector in the orthogonal complement of $s$'s direction, then the orthogonal projection of $p + v$ onto $s$ equals $p$.\n</tool_response>\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"web_search\", \"arguments\": {\"query\": \"angle between line and plane definition geometry\", \"num_results\": 5}}\n</tool_call>\n<tool_response>\n[1] Angle Between a Line & a Plane - IB Maths AA Revision Notes\n    The angle between a line and a plane is defined to be the angle between: The projection of the line onto the plane.\n    https://www.savemyexams.com/dp/maths/ib/aa/21/hl/revision-notes/geometry-and-trigonometry/vector-planes/angles-between-lines-and-planes/\n[2] Angle between a Line and a Plane\n    In analytic geometry, the angle between the line and the plane is equivalent to the complement of the angle between the line and the normal.\n    https://byjus.com/maths/angle-between-line-plane/\n[3] IB The Angle Between a Line and a Plane Lesson\n    In this lesson we're going to use trigonometry and coordinate geometry to find the angle between a line and a plane.\n    https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=EH8rBOO0MeM\n[4] Formula for the angle between say a plane to a plane or line to line : r/askmath\n    Is the formula for the angle between say a plane to a plane or line to line:\n\n* sin(theta) = |a•b| / |a||b|\n\nOR\n\n2. sin(theta) = a•b / |a||b|\n\nEssentially ...\n    https://www.reddit.com/r/askmath/comments/1b0cxm5/formula_for_the_angle_between_say_a_plane_to_a/\n[5] Understanding the Angle Between a Line and a Plane in Geometry\n    The angle between a line and a plane measures the amount of inclination between them at any given point of intersection.\n    https://www.intmath.com/functions-and-graphs/understanding-the-angle-between-a-line-and-a-plane-in-geometry.php\n</tool_response>\nNow I understand the problem better. The angle between a line and a plane is the angle between the line and its orthogonal projection onto the plane. Let me formalize this in Lean 4.\n\nThe key insight is:\n- For a point M in the plane π, the line AM forms an angle with π equal to the angle between AM and its projection onto π\n- This angle equals the angle between AM and the vector from M to the orthogonal projection of A onto π\n- Similarly for BM\n\nLet me write the formal statement:\n\n```lean4\nimport Mathlib\n\nopen AffineSubspace EuclideanGeometry\n\n/-- \nXXIII OM - I - Problem 4: \nGiven two points A and B not in a plane π, find the set of all points M in π \nsuch that the lines AM and BM form equal angles with the plane π.\n\nThe angle between a line and a plane is the angle between the line and its \northogonal projection onto the plane. This equals the angle at M between \nthe vectors MA and M(proj_π(A)), where proj_π(A) is the orthogonal projection \nof A onto π.\n-/\ntheorem xxiii_om_i_problem_4 \n  {P : Type*} [NormedAddCommGroup P] [InnerProductSpace ℝ P] [CompleteSpace P]\n  (π : AffineSubspace ℝ P) [π.direction.HasOrthogonalProjection] [Nonempty π]\n  (A B : P) (hA : A ∉ π) (hB : B ∉ π) :\n  {M : P | M ∈ π ∧ ∠ A M (orthogonalProjection π A) = ∠ B M (orthogonalProjection π B)} = \n  {M : P | M ∈ π ∧ dist A (orthogonalProjection π A) / dist A M = dist B (orthogonalProjection π B) / dist B M} := by sorry\n```\n\n# Round 2 (Semantic Self-validation)\n\nYour brief analysis:\n1. Math Assertion Analysis: \n   - Given: Plane π, points A, B ∉ π\n   - Find: Set of points M ∈ π where lines AM and BM form equal angles with plane π\n   - Angle definition: angle between line and plane = angle between line and its orthogonal projection onto plane\n\n2. Lean Statement Analysis (Proof Ignored):\n   - Type P: NormedAddCommGroup with InnerProductSpace ℝ P structure\n   - π: AffineSubspace with orthogonal projection capability\n   - A, B: Points in P with constraints hA: A ∉ π, hB: B ∉ π\n   - Theorem equates two set descriptions:\n     * Set 1: M ∈ π with ∠ A M (orthogonalProjection π A) = ∠ B M (orthogonalProjection π B)\n     * Set 2: M ∈ π with distance ratio equality\n\n3. Comparative Verification:\n   - Variables and constraints match (A, B ∉ π; M ∈ π)\n   - Angle condition correctly formalized using orthogonal projections\n   - The theorem provides a characterization of the solution set (appropriate for \"find the set\" problems)\n   - Distance ratio formulation is mathematically equivalent to angle equality (via trigonometric relationships)\n   - Proper Lean syntax with ∈ notation, correct type structure\n\n4. Conclusion: The Lean statement correctly formalizes the mathematical problem by characterizing the solution set through an equivalent condition.\n\n5. Accuracy Confirmation: All elements match - the formal statement captures the geometric constraints, correctly represents the angle condition using orthogonal projections, and provides a valid characterization of the solution set. The distance ratio equivalence is a standard geometric result for this type of problem.\n\nResult: Correct\n\nTherefore, the outcome of the consistency verification is: Correct\n\n</round>\n\n</think>\n\n<answer>\n\n```lean4\nimport Mathlib\n\nopen AffineSubspace EuclideanGeometry\n\n/-- \nXXIII OM - I - Problem 4: \nGiven two points A and B not in a plane π, find the set of all points M in π \nsuch that the lines AM and BM form equal angles with the plane π.\n\nThe angle between a line and a plane is the angle between the line and its \northogonal projection onto the plane. This equals the angle at M between \nthe vectors MA and M(proj_π(A)), where proj_π(A) is the orthogonal projection \nof A onto π.\n-/\ntheorem xxiii_om_i_problem_4 \n  {P : Type*} [NormedAddCommGroup P] [InnerProductSpace ℝ P] [CompleteSpace P]\n  (π : AffineSubspace ℝ P) [π.direction.HasOrthogonalProjection] [Nonempty π]\n  (A B : P) (hA : A ∉ π) (hB : B ∉ π) :\n  {M : P | M ∈ π ∧ ∠ A M (orthogonalProjection π A) = ∠ B M (orthogonalProjection π B)} = \n  {M : P | M ∈ π ∧ dist A (orthogonalProjection π A) / dist A M = dist B (orthogonalProjection π B) / dist B M} := by sorry\n```\n\n</answer>"}], "informal_statement": "XXIII OM - I - Problem 4\n\nPoints $ A $ and $ B $ do not belong to the plane $ \\pi $. Find the set of all points $ M \\in \\pi $ with the property that the lines $ AM $ and $ BM $ form equal angles with the plane $ \\pi $.", "num_rounds": 2, "num_tool_calls": 6, "tools_used": ["leansearch", "web_search"], "success": true, "syntax_pass": true, "syntax_rewrites": [], "retrieval_domain": "math", "dataset_name": "numina15_olympiads", "question_id": "olympiads_994"}
{"messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "Think step by step to translate the mathematical problem in natural language to Lean 4, and verify the consistency.\nLet  $n\\geqslant 3$  be an integer. Prove that there exists a set  $S$  of  $2n$  positive integers satisfying the following property: For every  $m=2,3,...,n$  the set  $S$  can be partitioned into two subsets with equal sums of elements, with one of subsets of cardinality  $m$ ."}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "<think>\n# Round 1 (Autoformalization)\n\n<round>\n\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"leansearch\", \"arguments\": {\"query\": \"finite set partition two subsets equal sum cardinality\", \"num_results\": 8}}\n</tool_call>\n<tool_response>\n[1] Finpartition.sum_card_parts  (theorem)\n    : ∑ i ∈ P.parts, #i = #s\n    For a finite partition $ P $ of a set $ s $, the sum of the cardinalities of the parts of $ P $ equals the cardinality of $ s $, i.e., $\\sum_{i \\in P.\\text{parts}} \\#i = \\#s$.\n[2] Finset.exists_disjoint_union_of_even_card  (theorem)\n    [DecidableEq α] {s : Finset α} (he : Even #s) : ∃ (t u : Finset α), t ∪ u = s ∧ Disjoint t u ∧ #t = #u\n    For any type $\\alpha$ with decidable equality, if a finite set $s$ has even cardinality, then there exist finite subsets $t$ and $u$ of $\\alpha$ such that $t \\cup u = s$, $t$ and $u$ are disjoint, and $|t| = |u|$.\n[3] Set.exists_union_disjoint_ncard_eq_of_even  (theorem)\n    (he : Even s.ncard) : ∃ (t u : Set α), t ∪ u = s ∧ Disjoint t u ∧ t.ncard = u.ncard\n    For any set $ s $ with even cardinality, there exist disjoint subsets $ t $ and $ u $ such that $ t \\cup u = s $ and $ |t| = |u| $.\n[4] Set.exists_union_disjoint_cardinal_eq_iff  (theorem)\n    (s : Set α) : Even (s.ncard) ↔ ∃ (t u : Set α), t ∪ u = s ∧ Disjoint t u ∧ #t = #u\n    For any set $ s $, the natural cardinality $ \\#s $ is even if and only if there exist subsets $ t $ and $ u $ such that $ t \\cup u = s $, $ t $ and $ u $ are disjoint, and $ \\#t = \\#u $.\n[5] Finpartition.card_parts_equitabilise  (theorem)\n    (hm : m ≠ 0) : #(P.equitabilise h).parts = a + b\n    If $ m \\neq 0 $, then the cardinality of the parts of the equitable partition $ P.equitabilise h $ is equal to $ a + b $.\n[6] Finset.exists_disjoint_union_of_even_card_iff  (theorem)\n    [DecidableEq α] (s : Finset α) : Even #s ↔ ∃ (t u : Finset α), t ∪ u = s ∧ Disjoint t u ∧ #t = #u\n    A finite set $ s $ has even cardinality if and only if there exist two disjoint finite subsets $ t $ and $ u $ such that $ t \\cup u = s $ and $ |t| = |u| $.\n[7] Finpartition.IsEquipartition.card_parts_eq_average  (theorem)\n    (hP : P.IsEquipartition) (ht : t ∈ P.parts) : #t = #s / #P.parts ∨ #t = #s / #P.parts + 1\n    For an equitable finite partition $ P $ of a set $ s $, each part $ t $ in $ P $ has cardinality equal to $ \\frac{|s|}{|P|} $ or $ \\frac{|s|}{|P|} + 1 $, where $ |s| $ denotes the cardinality of $ s $ and $ |P| $ denotes the number of parts in $ P $.\n[8] Set.exists_union_disjoint_cardinal_eq_of_even  (theorem)\n    (he : Even s.ncard) : ∃ (t u : Set α), t ∪ u = s ∧ Disjoint t u ∧ #t = #u\n    For any set $ s $ over a type $ \\alpha $, if the natural cardinality of $ s $ is even, then there exist two disjoint subsets $ t $ and $ u $ of $ \\alpha $ such that their union is $ s $ and each has the same cardinality as the other.\n</tool_response>\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"leansearch\", \"arguments\": {\"query\": \"Finset sum elements positive integers\", \"num_results\": 8}}\n</tool_call>\n<tool_response>\n[1] Finset.sum_pos  (theorem)\n    ∀ {ι : Type u_1} {M : Type u_4} [inst : AddCommMonoid M] [inst_1 : PartialOrder M] [IsOrderedCancelAddMonoid M]\n  {f : ι → M} {s : Finset ι}, (∀ i ∈ s, 0 < f i) → s.Nonempty → 0 < ∑ i ∈ s, f i\n    Let $ M $ be an ordered cancellative additive commutative monoid, $ \\iota $ a type, $ s $ a non-empty finite set of elements of $ \\iota $, and $ f : \\iota \\to M $. If $ 0 < f(i) $ for all $ i \\in s $, then the sum $ \\sum_{i \\in s} f(i) $ satisfies $ 0 < \\sum_{i \\in s} f(i) $.\n[2] Finset.sum_pos_iff  (theorem)\n    ∀ {ι : Type u_1} {M : Type u_4} [inst : AddCommMonoid M] [inst_1 : PartialOrder M] [CanonicallyOrderedAdd M] {f : ι → M}\n  {s : Finset ι}, 0 < ∑ x ∈ s, f x ↔ ∃ x ∈ s, 0 < f x\n    For any finite set $ s $ and function $ f : \\iota \\to M $, where $ M $ is an additive commutative monoid equipped with a partial order and canonical order, the sum $ \\sum_{x \\in s} f(x) $ is positive if and only if there exists an element $ x \\in s $ such that $ f(x) $ is positive.\n[3] Finset.sum_pos'  (theorem)\n    ∀ {ι : Type u_1} {M : Type u_4} [inst : AddCommMonoid M] [inst_1 : PartialOrder M] [IsOrderedCancelAddMonoid M]\n  {f : ι → M} {s : Finset ι}, (∀ i ∈ s, 0 ≤ f i) → (∃ i ∈ s, 0 < f i) → 0 < ∑ i ∈ s, f i\n    For any finite set $s$ of elements of type $\\iota$ and a function $f : \\iota \\to M$ where $M$ is an ordered cancellative additive commutative monoid, if all $f(i)$ are non-negative ($0 \\leq f(i)$ for all $i \\in s$) and at least one $f(i)$ is positive ($0 < f(i)$ for some $i \\in s$), then the sum $\\sum_{i \\in s} f(i)$ is strictly positive ($0 < \\sum_{i \\in s} f(i)$).\n[4] Finset.sum_pos_iff_of_nonneg  (theorem)\n    ∀ {ι : Type u_1} {N : Type u_5} [inst : AddCommMonoid N] [inst_1 : PartialOrder N] {f : ι → N} {s : Finset ι}\n  [AddLeftMono N], (∀ x ∈ s, 0 ≤ f x) → (0 < ∑ x ∈ s, f x ↔ ∃ x ∈ s, 0 < f x)\n    For any finite set $ s $ of elements of type $ \\iota $ and a function $ f : \\iota \\to N $ where $ N $ is an additive commutative monoid equipped with a partial order and left addition monotonicity, if all $ f(x) $ for $ x \\in s $ are non-negative ($ 0 \\leq f(x) $), then the sum $ \\sum_{x \\in s} f(x) $ is positive ($ 0 < \\sum_{x \\in s} f(x) $) if and only if there exists an $ x \\in s $ such that $ f(x) $ is positive ($ 0 < f(x) $).\n[5] Finset.sum_nonneg  (theorem)\n    ∀ {ι : Type u_1} {N : Type u_5} [inst : AddCommMonoid N] [inst_1 : PartialOrder N] {f : ι → N} {s : Finset ι}\n  [AddLeftMono N], (∀ i ∈ s, 0 ≤ f i) → 0 ≤ ∑ i ∈ s, f i\n    Let $\\iota$ be an index type and $N$ an additive commutative monoid equipped with a partial order $\\leq$ where addition is left-monic. For any finite set $s \\subseteq \\iota$ and function $f : \\iota \\to N$, if $0 \\leq f(i)$ for all $i \\in s$, then the sum $\\sum_{i \\in s} f(i)$ satisfies $0 \\leq \\sum_{i \\in s} f(i)$.\n[6] Finset.sum_range  (theorem)\n    ∀ {M : Type u_2} [inst : AddCommMonoid M] {n : ℕ} (f : ℕ → M), ∑ i ∈ Finset.range n, f i = ∑ i, f ↑i\n    For any additive commutative monoid $ M $, natural number $ n $, and function $ f : \\mathbb{N} \\to M $, the sum of $ f(i) $ for $ i $ in the finite set $ \\{0, 1, \\ldots, n - 1\\} $ is equal to the sum of $ f(i) $ for all $ i $ in the finite type $ \\mathrm{Fin}(n) $, where each element $ i \\in \\mathrm{Fin}(n) $ is identified with its corresponding natural number.\n[7] Finset.sum_range_le_sum  (theorem)\n    {s : Finset ℤ} {c : ℤ} (hs : ∀ x ∈ s, c ≤ x) : ∑ n ∈ range #s, (c + n) ≤ ∑ x ∈ s, x\n    For any finite set $ s $ of integers and any integer $ c $ such that every element $ x \\in s $ satisfies $ c \\leq x $, the sum of $ c + n $ for $ n $ from $ 0 $ to $ |s| - 1 $ is less than or equal to the sum of the elements in $ s $. Formally, $ \\sum_{n=0}^{|s|-1} (c + n) \\leq \\sum_{x \\in s} x $.\n[8] Finset.sum  (definition)\n    {ι : Type u_1} → {M : Type u_3} → [AddCommMonoid M] → Finset ι → (ι → M) → M\n    Given a finite set $s$ of elements of type $\\iota$ and a function $f : \\iota \\to M$ where $M$ is an additive commutative monoid, this function returns the sum of $f(i)$ for all $i \\in s$. The sum is computed by converting the finite set to a multiset, applying the function to each element, and then summing the resulting multiset using the additive structure of $M$.\n</tool_response>\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"leansearch\", \"arguments\": {\"query\": \"Finset partition two subsets equal sum\", \"num_results\": 8}}\n</tool_call>\n<tool_response>\n[1] Finset.exists_disjoint_union_of_even_card  (theorem)\n    [DecidableEq α] {s : Finset α} (he : Even #s) : ∃ (t u : Finset α), t ∪ u = s ∧ Disjoint t u ∧ #t = #u\n    For any type $\\alpha$ with decidable equality, if a finite set $s$ has even cardinality, then there exist finite subsets $t$ and $u$ of $\\alpha$ such that $t \\cup u = s$, $t$ and $u$ are disjoint, and $|t| = |u|$.\n[2] Finset.sum_partition  (theorem)\n    ∀ {ι : Type u_1} {M : Type u_4} {s : Finset ι} [inst : AddCommMonoid M] {f : ι → M} (R : Setoid ι)\n  [inst_1 : DecidableRel ⇑R], ∑ x ∈ s, f x = ∑ xbar ∈ Finset.image (Quotient.mk R) s, ∑ y ∈ s with ⟦y⟧ = xbar, f y\n    For any finite set $s$ of elements of type $\\iota$, a function $f : \\iota \\to M$ into an additive commutative monoid $M$, and an equivalence relation $R$ on $\\iota$, the sum of $f(x)$ over all $x \\in s$ equals the sum over each equivalence class $[x]$ of $s$ under $R$ of the sum of $f(y)$ for all $y \\in s$ in that class. Formally, $$\\sum_{x \\in s} f(x) = \\sum_{[x] \\in s/R} \\sum_{\\substack{y \\in s \\\\ y \\sim [x]}} f(y).$$\n[3] Nat.Partition.ofSums.congr_simp  (theorem)\n    ∀ (n : ℕ) (l l_1 : Multiset ℕ) (e_l : l = l_1) (hl : l.sum = n),\n  Nat.Partition.ofSums n l hl = Nat.Partition.ofSums n l_1 ⋯\n    If two multisets of natural numbers are equal and their sum is $ n $, then the partitions of $ n $ constructed by removing zeros from these multisets are equal.\n[4] Nat.Partition.mk.congr_simp  (theorem)\n    ∀ {n : ℕ} (parts parts_1 : Multiset ℕ) (e_parts : parts = parts_1) (parts_pos : ∀ {i : ℕ}, i ∈ parts → 0 < i)\n  (parts_sum : parts.sum = n),\n  { parts := parts, parts_pos := parts_pos, parts_sum := parts_sum } =\n    { parts := parts_1, parts_pos := ⋯, parts_sum := ⋯ }\n    Given a natural number $n$, if two multisets $parts$ and $parts_1$ of positive integers are equal and both sum to $n$, then the partitions constructed from these multisets are equal. Formally, if $parts = parts_1$ and $parts.sum = n$, then ${ parts := parts, parts_pos, parts_sum } = { parts := parts_1, parts_pos, parts_sum }$ as partitions of $n$.\n[5] Finpartition.sum_card_parts  (theorem)\n    : ∑ i ∈ P.parts, #i = #s\n    For a finite partition $ P $ of a set $ s $, the sum of the cardinalities of the parts of $ P $ equals the cardinality of $ s $, i.e., $\\sum_{i \\in P.\\text{parts}} \\#i = \\#s$.\n[6] Finpartition.mk.congr_simp  (theorem)\n    ∀ {α : Type u_1} [inst : Lattice α] [inst_1 : OrderBot α] {a : α} (parts parts_1 : Finset α) (e_parts : parts = parts_1)\n  (supIndep : parts.SupIndep id) (sup_parts : parts.sup id = a) (bot_notMem : ⊥ ∉ parts),\n  { parts := parts, supIndep := supIndep, sup_parts := sup_parts, bot_notMem := bot_notMem } =\n    { parts := parts_1, supIndep := ⋯, sup_parts := ⋯, bot_notMem := ⋯ }\n    In a lattice $\\alpha$ with a bottom element $\\bot$, if two finite sets $\\text{parts}$ and $\\text{parts}_1$ are equal, both satisfy supremum-independence (each element is disjoint from the supremum of any subset not containing it), their supremum equals an element $a \\in \\alpha$, and neither set contains $\\bot$, then the finite partitions constructed from these sets are equal. Formally, if $\\text{parts} = \\text{parts}_1$, $\\text{parts}.\\text{SupIndep}(\\text{id})$, $\\text{parts}.\\sup(\\text{id}) = a$, and $\\bot \\notin \\text{parts}$, then the corresponding $\\text{Finpartition}$ instances are equal...\n[7] Setoid.IsPartition.finpartition_parts  (theorem)\n    ∀ {α : Type u_1} {c : Finset (Set α)} (hc : Setoid.IsPartition ↑c), hc.finpartition.parts = c\n    If $ c $ is a finite set of subsets of a type $ \\alpha $ that forms a partition (i.e., the subsets are non-empty, pairwise disjoint, and their union is the universal set $ \\alpha $), then the parts of the finite partition constructed from $ c $ are exactly the set $ c $ itself.\n[8] OrderedFinpartition.sum_sigma_eq_sum  (theorem)\n    ∀ {n : ℕ} (c : OrderedFinpartition n) {α : Type u_5} [inst : AddCommMonoid α] (v : Fin n → α),\n  ∑ m, ∑ r, v (c.emb m r) = ∑ i, v i\n    For any natural number $n$, an ordered finite partition $c$ of $n$, and a function $v : \\mathrm{Fin}(n) \\to \\alpha$ where $\\alpha$ is an additive commutative monoid, the double sum over the partition indices $m$ and $r$ of $v(c.\\mathrm{emb}(m, r))$ equals the total sum over all $i \\in \\mathrm{Fin}(n)$ of $v(i)$. This establishes that summing over the partitioned structure is equivalent to summing over the original finite set.\n</tool_response>\nNow I have enough information to formalize the statement. Let me translate the problem:\n\n1. We need an integer $n \\geq 3$\n2. We need to prove existence of a set $S$ of $2n$ positive integers\n3. For every $m$ from $2$ to $n$, the set $S$ can be partitioned into two subsets with equal sums, where one subset has cardinality $m$\n\nKey points:\n- $S$ is a finite set of positive integers with cardinality $2n$\n- For each $m \\in \\{2, 3, ..., n\\}$, there exist disjoint subsets $A, B$ such that $A \\cup B = S$, $\\sum A = \\sum B$, and $|A| = m$ (which implies $|B| = 2n - m$)\n\n```lean4\nimport Mathlib\n\ntheorem exists_set_partition_equal_sum (n : ℕ) (hn : 3 ≤ n) :\n  ∃ (S : Finset ℕ), (∀ x ∈ S, 0 < x) ∧ S.card = 2 * n ∧\n    ∀ m ∈ Finset.Icc 2 n, ∃ (A B : Finset ℕ),\n      A ⊆ S ∧ B ⊆ S ∧ A ∪ B = S ∧ Disjoint A B ∧\n      A.card = m ∧ (∑ x ∈ A, x) = (∑ x ∈ B, x) := by sorry\n```\n\n# Round 1 (Semantic Self-validation)\n\nYour brief analysis:\n1. Math Assertion Analysis: The problem requires proving the existence of a set S of 2n positive integers (n ≥ 3) such that for every m ∈ {2, 3, ..., n}, S can be partitioned into two subsets with equal sums, where one subset has cardinality m. Key components include: n ≥ 3, S has 2n positive integers, partition into two subsets for each m ∈ [2, n], subset cardinality m, equal sums.\n2. Lean Statement Analysis (Proof Ignored): The theorem states ∃ S : Finset ℕ with S.card = 2n, all elements positive (∀ x ∈ S, 0 < x), and for all m ∈ Finset.Icc 2 n, there exist disjoint subsets A, B ⊆ S with A ∪ B = S, A.card = m, and ∑ A = ∑ B. The Lean code uses Finset.Icc 2 n to represent m ∈ {2, ..., n}, and correctly enforces the partition conditions.\n3. Comparative Verification: The Lean statement matches the problem's requirements. The set S has 2n positive integers (via S.card = 2n and 0 < x). The ∀ m ∈ Finset.Icc 2 n quantifier aligns with m = 2, ..., n. The partition conditions (A ⊆ S, B ⊆ S, A ∪ B = S, Disjoint A B) ensure a valid partition. The requirement A.card = m captures the problem's \"one subset of cardinality m\" (since B's cardinality is 2n - m, which is not constrained). The sum equality (∑ A = ∑ B) matches the problem's requirement.\n4. Conclusion: The Lean statement correctly formalizes the problem's requirements.\n5. Accuracy Confirmation: All components match. The Lean code correctly captures the existence of S with 2n positive integers, the partition conditions for each m ∈ {2, ..., n}, and the sum equality. The use of Finset.Icc 2 n correctly represents the range of m. The requirement A.card = m aligns with the problem's \"one subset of cardinality m\" since the other subset's size is determined by the partition.\n\nResult: Correct\n\nTherefore, the outcome of the consistency verification is: Correct\n\n</round>\n\n</think>\n\n<answer>\n\n```lean4\nimport Mathlib\n\ntheorem exists_set_partition_equal_sum (n : ℕ) (hn : 3 ≤ n) :\n  ∃ (S : Finset ℕ), (∀ x ∈ S, 0 < x) ∧ S.card = 2 * n ∧\n    ∀ m ∈ Finset.Icc 2 n, ∃ (A B : Finset ℕ),\n      A ⊆ S ∧ B ⊆ S ∧ A ∪ B = S ∧ Disjoint A B ∧\n      A.card = m ∧ (∑ x ∈ A, x) = (∑ x ∈ B, x) := by sorry\n```\n\n</answer>"}], "informal_statement": "Let  $n\\geqslant 3$  be an integer. Prove that there exists a set  $S$  of  $2n$  positive integers satisfying the following property: For every  $m=2,3,...,n$  the set  $S$  can be partitioned into two subsets with equal sums of elements, with one of subsets of cardinality  $m$ .", "num_rounds": 1, "num_tool_calls": 3, "tools_used": ["leansearch"], "success": true, "syntax_pass": true, "syntax_rewrites": [], "retrieval_domain": "math", "dataset_name": "aops", "question_id": "16574"}
