y $, if $ x < y $, then $ e^x < e^y $.\n[4] EReal.coe_strictMono  (theorem)\n    : StrictMono Real.toEReal\n    The canonical embedding from the real numbers $\\mathbb{R}$ to the extended real numbers $\\overline{\\mathbb{R}}$ is strictly monotonic, meaning that for all real numbers $a, b$, if $a < b$ then $\\text{Real.toEReal}(a) < \\text{Real.toEReal}(b)$ in the extended real number ordering.\n[5] Real.sinh_sub_id_strictMono  (theorem)\n    : StrictMono fun x => sinh x - x\n    The function $ f(x) = \\sinh(x) - x $ is strictly monotonic, meaning that for all real numbers $ a $ and $ b $, if $ a < b $, then $ f(a) < f(b) $.\n[6] Real.strictMonoOn_one_add_div_one_sub  (theorem)\n    : StrictMonoOn (fun (x : ℝ) => (1 + x) / (1 - x)) (Ioo (-1) 1)\n    The function $ f(x) = \\frac{1 + x}{1 - x} $ is strictly increasing on the open interval $ (-1, 1) $.\n[7] strictMono_of_hasDerivAt_pos  (theorem)\n    {f f' : ℝ → ℝ} (hf : ∀ x, HasDerivAt f (f' x) x) (hf' : ∀ x, 0 < f' x) : StrictMono f\n    If a function $ f : \\mathbb{R} \\to \\mathbb{R} $ is differentiable everywhere with derivative $ f'(x) > 0 $ for all $ x \\in \\mathbb{R} $, then $ f $ is strictly monotonic (i.e., strictly increasing).\n[8] StrictMonoOn  (definition)\n    (f : α → β) (s : Set α) : Prop\n    A function $ f $ is strictly monotonic on a set $ s $ if for all $ a, b \\in s $, whenever $ a < b $, it follows that $ f(a) < f(b) $.\n</tool_response>\nNow I have enough information to formalize this problem. Let me write the Lean 4 statement:\n\n```lean4\nimport Mathlib\n\n/-- \nLet f be a strictly increasing function on ℝ. Define g(x,t) = (f(x+t) - f(x)) / (f(x) - f(x-t))\nfor real x and positive t. Given that 1/2 < g(0,t) < 2 for all t > 0, and\n1/2 < g(x,t) < 2 for all t ≤ |x| when x ≠ 0, prove that 1/14 < g(x,t) < 14\nfor all real x and positive t.\n-/\ntheorem fin3_inequality_bound (f : ℝ → ℝ) (hf : StrictMono f) :\n  let g := fun (x : ℝ) (t : ℝ) => (f (x + t) - f x) / (f x - f (x - t))\n  (∀ t : ℝ, 0 <