{"messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "Think step by step to translate the mathematical problem in natural language to Lean 4, and verify the consistency.\nThe graphs of  $y=\\log_3x$ ,  $y=\\log_x3$ ,  $y=\\log_{\\frac13}x$ , and  $y=\\log_x\\frac13$  are plotted on the same set of axes. How many points in the plane with positive  $x-$ coordinates lie on two or more of the graphs? $\\textbf{(A) } 2 \\qquad\\textbf{(B) } 3 \\qquad\\textbf{(C) } 4 \\qquad\\textbf{(D) } 5 \\qquad\\textbf{(E) } 6$"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "<think>\n# Round 1 (Autoformalization)\n\n<round>\n\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"leansearch\", \"arguments\": {\"query\": \"logarithm function Real.logb base change of base\", \"num_results\": 10}}\n</tool_call>\n<tool_response>\n[1] Real.logb  (definition)\n    (b x : ℝ) : ℝ\n    The function maps real numbers $ b $ and $ x $ to the quotient of the natural logarithm of the absolute value of $ x $ divided by the natural logarithm of the absolute value of $ b $, with each logarithm being zero when its argument is zero.\n[2] Real.div_logb  (theorem)\n    {a b c : ℝ} (h₁ : c ≠ 0) (h₂ : c ≠ 1) (h₃ : c ≠ -1) : logb a c / logb b c = logb a b\n    For real numbers $a, b, c$ with $c eq 0$, $c eq 1$, and $c eq -1$, the quotient of the logarithm of $a$ with base $c$ divided by the logarithm of $b$ with base $c$ equals the logarithm of $a$ with base $b$, i.e., $\\frac{\\log_c a}{\\log_c b} = \\log_b a$.\n[3] Real.log_div_log  (theorem)\n    : log x / log b = logb b x\n    For any real numbers $x$ and $b$, the quotient of the natural logarithm of $x$ divided by the natural logarithm of $b$ equals the logarithm of $x$ with base $b$, i.e., $\\frac{\\ln x}{\\ln b} = \\log_b x$.\n[4] Real.mul_logb  (theorem)\n    {a b c : ℝ} (h₁ : b ≠ 0) (h₂ : b ≠ 1) (h₃ : b ≠ -1) : logb a b * logb b c = logb a c\n    For real numbers $a$, $b$, and $c$ with $b eq 0$, $b eq 1$, and $b eq -1$, the product of the logarithm of $b$ with base $a$ and the logarithm of $c$ with base $b$ equals the logarithm of $c$ with base $a$, i.e., $\\log_a b \\cdot \\log_b c = \\log_a c$.\n[5] Real.rpow_logb  (theorem)\n    (hx : 0 < x) : b ^ logb b x = x\n    For any real number $ b $ and $ x > 0 $, it holds that $ b^{\\log_b x} = x $.\n[6] Real.inv_logb  (theorem)\n    (a b : ℝ) : (logb a b)⁻¹ = logb b a\n    For positive real numbers $ a $ and $ b $ with $ a \\neq 1 $ and $ b \\neq 1 $, the inverse of the logarithm of $ b $ with base $ a $ is equal to the logarithm of $ a $ with base $ b $, i.e., $ (\\log_a b)^{-1} = \\log_b a $.\n[7] Real.logb_div_base  (theorem)\n    {a b : ℝ} (h₁ : a ≠ 0) (h₂ : b ≠ 0) (c : ℝ) : logb (a / b) c = ((logb a c)⁻¹ - (logb b c)⁻¹)⁻¹\n    For non-zero real numbers $a$ and $b$, and any real number $c$, the logarithm of $c$ with base $a/b$ is equal to the reciprocal of the difference between the reciprocals of $\\log_a c$ and $\\log_b c$. In symbols, $\\log_{a/b} c = \\left( \\frac{1}{\\log_a c} - \\frac{1}{\\log_b c} \\right)^{-1}$.\n[8] Real.logb_neg_base_eq_logb  (theorem)\n    (b x : ℝ) : logb (-b) x = logb b x\n    For any real numbers $ b $ and $ x $, the logarithm base $-b$ of $ x $ is equal to the logarithm base $ b $ of $ x $, i.e., $ \\log_{-b} x = \\log_b x $.\n[9] Real.logb_eq_iff_rpow_eq  (theorem)\n    (hy : 0 < y) : logb b y = x ↔ b ^ x = y\n    For positive real numbers $b$ and $y$, the logarithm base $b$ of $y$ equals $x$ if and only if $b$ raised to the power of $x$ equals $y$. This establishes the fundamental equivalence between logarithmic and exponential forms for real numbers.\n[10] Real.logb_inv_base  (theorem)\n    (b x : ℝ) : logb b⁻¹ x = -logb b x\n    For any real numbers $b$ and $x$, the logarithm of $x$ with base $b^{-1}$ is equal to the negative of the logarithm of $x$ with base $b$, i.e., $\\log_{b^{-1}} x = -\\log_b x$.\n</tool_response>\nLooking at this problem, I need to:\n1. Define the four logarithmic functions\n2. Find points where at least two graphs intersect\n3. Count points with positive x-coordinates\n\nLet me search for the appropriate Lean 4 logarithm notation first.\n\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"leansearch\", \"arguments\": {\"query\": \"Real.logb logarithm base definition domain\", \"num_results\": 8}}\n</tool_call>\n<tool_response>\n[1] Real.logb  (definition)\n    (b x : ℝ) : ℝ\n    The function maps real numbers $ b $ and $ x $ to the quotient of the natural logarithm of the absolute value of $ x $ divided by the natural logarithm of the absolute value of $ b $, with each logarithm being zero when its argument is zero.\n[2] Real.logb_self_eq_one_iff  (theorem)\n    : logb b b = 1 ↔ b ≠ 0 ∧ b ≠ 1 ∧ b ≠ -1\n    The logarithm base $ b $ of $ b $ equals 1 if and only if $ b $ is not equal to 0, 1, or -1. This accounts for the domain restrictions of the logarithm function where the base must be positive and not equal to 1, with additional exclusion of $ b = -1 $ due to the definition involving absolute values and real-number constraints.\n[3] Real.logb_eq_iff_rpow_eq  (theorem)\n    (hy : 0 < y) : logb b y = x ↔ b ^ x = y\n    For positive real numbers $b$ and $y$, the logarithm base $b$ of $y$ equals $x$ if and only if $b$ raised to the power of $x$ equals $y$. This establishes the fundamental equivalence between logarithmic and exponential forms for real numbers.\n[4] Real.logb_nonneg_iff  (theorem)\n    (hx : 0 < x) : 0 ≤ logb b x ↔ 1 ≤ x\n    For any positive real number $x$ and any positive real number $b \\ne 1$, the logarithm of $x$ with base $b$ is non-negative if and only if $x \\ge 1$.\n[5] Real.logb_injOn_pos_of_base_lt_one  (theorem)\n    : Set.InjOn (logb b) (Set.Ioi 0)\n    For any real number $ b $ satisfying $ 0 < b < 1 $, the logarithm function $ \\log_b(x) $ is injective on the domain $ x > 0 $. This means that if $ \\log_b(x) = \\log_b(y) $ for positive real numbers $ x $ and $ y $, then necessarily $ x = y $.\n[6] Real.logb_neg_eq_logb  (theorem)\n    (b x : ℝ) : logb b (-x) = logb b x\n    For any real numbers $ b $ and $ x $, the base-$ b $ logarithm of $ -x $ is equal to the base-$ b $ logarithm of $ x $, i.e., $ \\log_b(-x) = \\log_b(x) $.\n[7] Real.logb_nonpos_iff  (theorem)\n    (hx : 0 < x) : logb b x ≤ 0 ↔ x ≤ 1\n    For any positive real number $b \\neq 1$ and for any positive real number $x$, the logarithm base $b$ of $x$ satisfies $\\log_b x \\leq 0$ if and only if $x \\leq 1$.\n[8] Real.logb_nonpos_iff'  (theorem)\n    (hx : 0 ≤ x) : logb b x ≤ 0 ↔ x ≤ 1\n    For any real number $ x $ with $ x \\geq 0 $, $ \\log_b x \\leq 0 $ if and only if $ x \\leq 1 $.\n</tool_response>\nNow I have the information I need. The four functions are:\n- f₁(x) = log₃(x)\n- f₂(x) = logₓ(3)\n- f₃(x) = log_{1/3}(x)\n- f₄(x) = logₓ(1/3)\n\nI need to count points (x, y) with x > 0 that lie on at least 2 of these graphs.\n\n```lean4\nimport Mathlib\n\nopen Real\n\n/-- \nThe graphs of y = log₃(x), y = logₓ(3), y = log_{1/3}(x), and y = logₓ(1/3) are plotted.\nThis theorem states that there are exactly 3 points with positive x-coordinates \nthat lie on two or more of these graphs.\n-/\ntheorem log_graphs_intersection_count : \n  let f1 : ℝ → ℝ := fun x => logb 3 x\n  let f2 : ℝ → ℝ := fun x => logb x 3\n  let f3 : ℝ → ℝ := fun x => logb (1/3) x\n  let f4 : ℝ → ℝ := fun x => logb x (1/3)\n  let S : Set ℝ := {x : ℝ | x > 0 ∧ \n    ((f1 x = f2 x) ∨ (f1 x = f3 x) ∨ (f1 x = f4 x) ∨ \n     (f2 x = f3 x) ∨ (f2 x = f4 x) ∨ (f3 x = f4 x))}\n  Set.Finite S ∧ Set.ncard S = 3 := by sorry\n```\n\n[syntax_rewrite] unicode subscript digits → ASCII\n```lean4\nimport Mathlib\n\nopen Real\n\n/-- \nThe graphs of y = log3(x), y = logₓ(3), y = log_{1/3}(x), and y = logₓ(1/3) are plotted.\nThis theorem states that there are exactly 3 points with positive x-coordinates \nthat lie on two or more of these graphs.\n-/\ntheorem log_graphs_intersection_count : \n  let f1 : ℝ → ℝ := fun x => logb 3 x\n  let f2 : ℝ → ℝ := fun x => logb x 3\n  let f3 : ℝ → ℝ := fun x => logb (1/3) x\n  let f4 : ℝ → ℝ := fun x => logb x (1/3)\n  let S : Set ℝ := {x : ℝ | x > 0 ∧ \n    ((f1 x = f2 x) ∨ (f1 x = f3 x) ∨ (f1 x = f4 x) ∨ \n     (f2 x = f3 x) ∨ (f2 x = f4 x) ∨ (f3 x = f4 x))}\n  Set.Finite S ∧ Set.ncard S = 3 := by sorry\n```\n\n# Round 1 (Semantic Self-validation)\n\n1. Math Assertion Analysis: The problem asks for the number of points with positive x-coordinates where at least two of the four logarithmic functions intersect. The functions are y=log₃x, y=logₓ3, y=log_{1/3}x, and y=logₓ(1/3). The answer options suggest the count is between 2 and 6.\n\n2. Lean Statement Analysis (Proof Ignored): The Lean code defines four functions f1-f4 corresponding to the given logarithmic equations. The set S collects all x>0 where any pair of functions are equal. The theorem claims S has exactly 3 elements.\n\n3. Comparative Verification: \n   - Functions are correctly formalized using logb with appropriate bases and arguments.\n   - The set S correctly captures intersections between any two functions via pairwise equality checks.\n   - The domain x>0 matches the problem's requirement for positive x-coordinates.\n   - The theorem's conclusion (cardinality 3) aligns with one of the answer choices (B).\n\n4. Conclusion: The Lean formalization accurately represents the problem's structure, constraints, and required count.\n\n5. Accuracy Confirmation: All components match perfectly. The functions, domain, intersection conditions, and cardinality claim are all correctly formalized. The answer choice (3) is one of the provided options, so the formalization is faithful to the problem's question.\n\nResult: Correct\n\nTherefore, the outcome of the consistency verification is: Correct\n\n</round>\n\n</think>\n\n<answer>\n\n```lean4\nimport Mathlib\n\nopen Real\n\n/-- \nThe graphs of y = log3(x), y = logₓ(3), y = log_{1/3}(x), and y = logₓ(1/3) are plotted.\nThis theorem states that there are exactly 3 points with positive x-coordinates \nthat lie on two or more of these graphs.\n-/\ntheorem log_graphs_intersection_count : \n  let f1 : ℝ → ℝ := fun x => logb 3 x\n  let f2 : ℝ → ℝ := fun x => logb x 3\n  let f3 : ℝ → ℝ := fun x => logb (1/3) x\n  let f4 : ℝ → ℝ := fun x => logb x (1/3)\n  let S : Set ℝ := {x : ℝ | x > 0 ∧ \n    ((f1 x = f2 x) ∨ (f1 x = f3 x) ∨ (f1 x = f4 x) ∨ \n     (f2 x = f3 x) ∨ (f2 x = f4 x) ∨ (f3 x = f4 x))}\n  Set.Finite S ∧ Set.ncard S = 3 := by sorry\n```\n\n</answer>"}], "informal_statement": "The graphs of  $y=\\log_3x$ ,  $y=\\log_x3$ ,  $y=\\log_{\\frac13}x$ , and  $y=\\log_x\\frac13$  are plotted on the same set of axes. How many points in the plane with positive  $x-$ coordinates lie on two or more of the graphs? $\\textbf{(A) } 2 \\qquad\\textbf{(B) } 3 \\qquad\\textbf{(C) } 4 \\qquad\\textbf{(D) } 5 \\qquad\\textbf{(E) } 6$", "num_rounds": 1, "num_tool_calls": 2, "tools_used": ["leansearch"], "success": true, "syntax_pass": true, "syntax_rewrites": ["unicode subscript digits → ASCII"], "retrieval_domain": "math", "dataset_name": "aops", "question_id": "14952"}
{"messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "Think step by step to translate the mathematical problem in natural language to Lean 4, and verify the consistency.\nConsider the set of all 6-digit numbers consisting of only three digits, $a,b,c$ where $a,b,c$ are distinct. Suppose the sum of all these numbers is $593999406$. What is the largest remainder when the three digit number $abc$ is divided by $100$?"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "<think>\n# Round 1 (Autoformalization)\n\n<round>\n\nLooking at this problem, I need to formalize a statement about 6-digit numbers formed from three distinct digits.\n\nLet me search for relevant mathematical concepts to help with the formalization.\n\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"leansearch\", \"arguments\": {\"query\": \"sum of numbers formed by digits combinatorics\", \"num_results\": 8}}\n</tool_call>\n<tool_response>\n[1] Nat.sum_digits_ofDigits_eq_sum  (theorem)\n    {b : ℕ} (hb : 1 < b) {l : ℕ} {L : List ℕ} (hL : L ∈ {L : List ℕ | L.length = l ∧ ∀ x ∈ L, x < b}) :\n  (b.digits (ofDigits b L)).sum = L.sum\n    For any base $ b > 1 $ and any list $ L $ of natural numbers where each element is less than $ b $, the sum of the digits of the number formed by interpreting $ L $ as a base-$ b $ number (with the first element as the least significant digit) is equal to the sum of the elements in $ L $. In symbols, $ \\sum (\\text{digits}_b(\\text{ofDigits}_b\\, L)) = \\sum L $.\n[2] Nat.sum_sum_digits_eq  (theorem)\n    {b : ℕ} (hb : 1 < b) (l : ℕ) : ∑ x ∈ Finset.range (b ^ l), (b.digits x).sum = l * b ^ (l - 1) * b.choose 2\n    For any natural number $ b > 1 $ and $ l $, the sum of the digit sums of all numbers from $ 0 $ to $ b^l - 1 $ in base $ b $ equals $ l \\cdot b^{l-1} \\cdot \\binom{b}{2} $.\n[3] Nat.ofDigits_eq_sum_mapIdx  (theorem)\n    (b : ℕ) (L : List ℕ) : ofDigits b L = (L.mapIdx fun i a => a * b ^ i).sum\n    For a natural number base $b$ and a list of natural numbers $L = [d_0, d_1, \\ldots, d_n]$, the value obtained by interpreting $L$ as digits in base $b$ (with $d_0$ as the least significant digit) is equal to the sum $\\sum_{i=0}^{n} d_i \\cdot b^i$.\n[4] List.sum_fixedLengthDigits_sum  (theorem)\n    {b : ℕ} (hb : 1 < b) (l : ℕ) : ∑ L ∈ fixedLengthDigits hb l, L.sum = l * b ^ (l - 1) * b.choose 2\n    For any natural number base $ b > 1 $ and length $ l $, the sum of the sums of all fixed-length digit lists of length $ l $ in base $ b $ (where each digit is strictly less than $ b $) is equal to $ l \\cdot b^{l - 1} \\cdot \\binom{b}{2} $.\n[5] Nat.sum_le_ofDigits  (theorem)\n    {p : ℕ} (L : List ℕ) (h : 1 ≤ p) : L.sum ≤ ofDigits p L\n    For any natural number $ p \\geq 1 $ and any list of natural numbers $ L = [d_0, d_1, \\ldots, d_n] $, the sum $ d_0 + d_1 + \\cdots + d_n $ is less than or equal to the number $ d_0 + p \\cdot d_1 + p^2 \\cdot d_2 + \\cdots + p^n \\cdot d_n $ obtained by interpreting $ L $ as digits in base $ p $.\n[6] Nat.ofDigits_digits_append_digits  (theorem)\n    {b m n : ℕ} : ofDigits b (digits b n ++ digits b m) = n + b ^ (digits b n).length * m\n    For any natural numbers $ b, m, n $, the value obtained by interpreting the concatenation of the base-$ b $ digits of $ n $ and $ m $ as a number in base $ b $ is equal to $ n + b^{ ext{length}( ext{digits}_b(n))} imes m $.\n[7] Nat.ofDigits_one  (theorem)\n    (L : List ℕ) : ofDigits 1 L = L.sum\n    Interpreting a list of digits in base 1 results in the sum of the digits, since each digit's place value is $1^k = 1$ for all positions $k$.\n[8] Nat.ofDigits_eq_sum_mapIdx_aux  (theorem)\n    (b : ℕ) (l : List ℕ) :\n  (l.zipWith ((fun a i : ℕ => a * b ^ (i + 1))) (List.range l.length)).sum =\n    b * (l.zipWith (fun a i => a * b ^ i) (List.range l.length)).sum\n    For a natural number base $ b $ and a list of digits $ l $, the sum of each digit multiplied by $ b $ raised to $ (i + 1) $, where $ i $ is the index, equals $ b $ multiplied by the sum of each digit multiplied by $ b^i $. Formally, $$\\sum_{i=0}^{\\text{length}(l)-1} l_i \\cdot b^{i+1} = b \\cdot \\sum_{i=0}^{\\text{length}(l)-1} l_i \\cdot b^i.$$\n</tool_response>\nLet me search for more specific concepts about permutations and digit arrangements:\n\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"leansearch\", \"arguments\": {\"query\": \"permutations of list distinct elements\", \"num_results\": 8}}\n</tool_call>\n<tool_response>\n[1] List.nodup_permutations  (theorem)\n    (s : List α) (hs : Nodup s) : Nodup s.permutations\n    If a list $ s $ has no duplicate elements, then the list of all permutations of $ s $ also has no duplicate permutations.\n[2] nodup_permsOfList  (theorem)\n    : ∀ {l : List α}, l.Nodup → (permsOfList l).Nodup\n    If a list $ l $ has no duplicate elements, then the list of permutations generated by $ \\text{permsOfList}\\, l $ also contains no duplicate permutations.\n[3] List.Nodup.cyclicPermutations  (theorem)\n    {l : List α} (hn : Nodup l) : Nodup (cyclicPermutations l)\n    If a list $ l $ has no duplicate elements, then the list of all cyclic permutations of $ l $ also contains no duplicates.\n[4] List.nodup_permutations_iff  (theorem)\n    {s : List α} : Nodup s.permutations ↔ Nodup s\n    The list of all permutations of a list $ s $ contains no duplicates if and only if $ s $ itself contains no duplicates.\n[5] List.Nodup.isCycleOn_formPerm  (theorem)\n    (h : l.Nodup) : l.formPerm.IsCycleOn {a | a ∈ l}\n    If $ l $ is a list with distinct elements, then the permutation constructed by composing transpositions of consecutive elements in $ l $ acts as a single cycle on the set of elements in $ l $.\n[6] mem_of_mem_permsOfList  (theorem)\n    : ∀ {l : List α} {f : Perm α}, f ∈ permsOfList l → {x : α} → f x ≠ x → x ∈ l\n    If a permutation $f$ belongs to the list of permutations generated by $\\text{permsOfList}\\, l$, then for any element $x$, the condition $f(x) \\neq x$ implies that $x$ is an element of the original list $l$.\n[7] Cycle.formPerm_coe  (theorem)\n    (l : List α) (hl : l.Nodup) : formPerm (l : Cycle α) hl = l.formPerm\n    For any list $ l $ of distinct elements in type $ \\alpha $, the permutation obtained by converting $ l $ to a cycle and applying the cycle's permutation function is equal to the permutation formed by composing transpositions of consecutive elements in $ l $.\n[8] List.permutations  (definition)\n    (l : List α) : List (List α)\n    The function returns the list of all permutations of the input list $ l $, constructed by recursively inserting elements into all possible positions.\n</tool_response>\nNow let me formalize this problem. I need to:\n\n1. Define the set of all 6-digit numbers formed from three distinct digits a, b, c\n2. State that their sum equals 593999406\n3. Ask for the largest remainder when abc (as a 3-digit number) is divided by 100\n\nLet me think about the structure:\n- We have three distinct digits a, b, c (each from 0-9, but since we're forming 6-digit numbers, at least one must be non-zero for the leading position)\n- We form all possible 6-digit numbers using only these three digits\n- Each position in the 6-digit number can be any of a, b, c\n- We need to find the maximum value of (100*a + 10*b + c) mod 100\n\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"leansearch\", \"arguments\": {\"query\": \"Finset product powers cartesian product\", \"num_results\": 8}}\n</tool_call>\n<tool_response>\n[1] Finset.product_pow  (theorem)\n    [Monoid β] (s : Finset α) (t : Finset β) : ∀ n, (s ×ˢ t) ^ n = (s ^ n) ×ˢ (t ^ n)\n    For any monoid $\\beta$ and finite sets $s \\subseteq \\alpha$ and $t \\subseteq \\beta$, the $n$th power of their Cartesian product $(s \\times t)^n$ is equal to the Cartesian product of their individual $n$th powers $s^n \\times t^n$, where exponentiation denotes repeated pointwise multiplication of sets.\n[2] Finset.prod_pow  (theorem)\n    (s : Finset ι) (n : ℕ) (f : ι → M) : ∏ x ∈ s, f x ^ n = (∏ x ∈ s, f x) ^ n\n    For any finite set $ s $, natural number $ n $, and function $ f : \\iota \\to M $ where $ M $ is a commutative monoid, it holds that $ \\prod_{x \\in s} f(x)^n = \\left( \\prod_{x \\in s} f(x) \\right)^n $.\n[3] NNReal.finset_prod_rpow  (theorem)\n    {ι} (s : Finset ι) (f : ι → ℝ≥0) (r : ℝ) : (∏ i ∈ s, f i ^ r) = (∏ i ∈ s, f i) ^ r\n    For any finite set $ s $ of indices, function $ f : s \\to \\mathbb{R}_{\\geq 0} $ mapping to non-negative real numbers, and real exponent $ r $, the product of $ f(i)^r $ over all $ i \\in s $ equals the $ r $-th power of the product of $ f(i) $ over $ s $. Symbolically: $\\prod_{i \\in s} f(i)^r = \\left(\\prod_{i \\in s} f(i)\\right)^r $.\n[4] Finset.product_eq_sprod  (theorem)\n    : Finset.product s t = s ×ˢ t\n    For any finite sets $ s $ and $ t $, the Cartesian product $ s \\times t $ is equal to the product constructed by the Finset.product function, i.e., $ \\mathrm{Finset.product}\\, s\\, t = s \\times t $.\n[5] Set.prod_pow  (theorem)\n    [Monoid β] (s : Set α) (t : Set β) : ∀ n, (s ×ˢ t) ^ n = (s ^ n) ×ˢ (t ^ n)\n    For any monoids $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$, and subsets $s \\subseteq \\alpha$ and $t \\subseteq \\beta$, the $n$-th power of the Cartesian product $s \\times t$ under pointwise multiplication satisfies $(s \\times t)^n = s^n \\times t^n$, where $s^n$ and $t^n$ denote the set powers defined by repeated multiplication in their respective monoids.\n[6] Finset.product  (definition)\n    (s : Finset α) (t : Finset β) : Finset (α × β)\n    The function that takes two finite sets $ s $ and $ t $ over types $ \\alpha $ and $ \\beta $, respectively, and returns their Cartesian product as a finite set of ordered pairs $ (a, b) $ where $ a \\in s $ and $ b \\in t $.\n[7] Finset.product_mul_product_comm  (theorem)\n    [DecidableEq β] (s₁ s₂ : Finset α) (t₁ t₂ : Finset β) : (s₁ ×ˢ t₁) * (s₂ ×ˢ t₂) = (s₁ * s₂) ×ˢ (t₁ * t₂)\n    For finite sets $s_1, s_2$ over type $\\alpha$ and $t_1, t_2$ over type $\\beta$ (with decidable equality on $\\beta$), the pointwise multiplication of their Cartesian products satisfies $(s_1 \\times t_1) \\cdot (s_2 \\times t_2) = (s_1 \\cdot s_2) \\times (t_1 \\cdot t_2)$, where $\\cdot$ denotes set multiplication and $\\times$ denotes Cartesian product.\n[8] Finset.instSProd  (instance)\n    : SProd (Finset α) (Finset β) (Finset (α × β))\n    For any types $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$, the Cartesian product of two finite sets over $\\alpha$ and $\\beta$ is itself a finite set consisting of ordered pairs $(a, b)$ where $a$ belongs to the first set and $b$ belongs to the second set. This instance establishes the canonical construction of such a product using the $\\mathrm{Finset.product}$ function.\n</tool_response>\nNow I have enough information to formalize this problem. Let me create the Lean 4 statement:\n\n```lean4\nimport Mathlib\n\nopen Finset List Nat\n\n/-- \nGiven three distinct digits a, b, c, consider all 6-digit numbers formed using only these digits.\nIf the sum of all such numbers equals 593999406, find the maximum remainder when the \n3-digit number abc is divided by 100.\n-/\ntheorem digit_sum_remainder_problem : \n  ∃ (a b c : ℕ), \n    -- a, b, c are distinct digits\n    a < 10 ∧ b < 10 ∧ c < 10 ∧\n    a ≠ b ∧ b ≠ c ∧ a ≠ c ∧\n    -- The sum of all 6-digit numbers formed from {a, b, c} equals 593999406\n    (∑ digits : Fin 6 → Fin 3, \n      let d := fun i => if digits i = 0 then a else if digits i = 1 then b else c\n      d 0 * 100000 + d 1 * 10000 + d 2 * 1000 + d 3 * 100 + d 4 * 10 + d 5) = 593999406 ∧\n    -- The maximum remainder when abc (as 3-digit number) is divided by 100\n    (∀ (a' b' c' : ℕ), \n      a' < 10 → b' < 10 → c' < 10 →\n      a' ≠ b' → b' ≠ c' → a' ≠ c' →\n      (∑ digits : Fin 6 → Fin 3, \n        let d := fun i => if digits i = 0 then a' else if digits i = 1 then b' else c'\n        d 0 * 100000 + d 1 * 10000 + d 2 * 1000 + d 3 * 100 + d 4 * 10 + d 5) = 593999406 →\n      (100 * a' + 10 * b' + c') % 100 ≤ (100 * a + 10 * b + c) % 100) := by sorry\n```\n\n# Round 1 (Semantic Self-validation)\n\nYour brief analysis:\n1. Math Assertion Analysis: \n   - Variables: a, b, c are distinct digits (0-9).\n   - 6-digit numbers must use only a, b, c, with first digit ≠ 0 (implicit constraint for 6-digit validity).\n   - Sum of all valid 6-digit numbers = 593,999,406.\n   - Target: Maximize (100a + 10b + c) mod 100 (equivalent to 10b + c).\n\n2. Lean Statement Analysis (Proof Ignored):\n   - Variables a, b, c are ℕ < 10, distinct.\n   - Sum includes all 3^6 combinations of a, b, c in 6 positions (including leading zeros if a/b/c=0).\n   - No constraint to exclude numbers starting with 0 (invalid 6-digit numbers).\n   - Correctly calculates remainder as (100a + 10b + c) % 100.\n   - Correctly asserts existence of a triplet with maximal remainder under the sum condition.\n\n3. Comparative Verification:\n   - **Critical Mismatch**: The Lean code allows first digit to be 0 (if a/b/c=0), violating 6-digit number validity. This invalidates the sum calculation.\n   - **Secondary Issue**: The problem implicitly requires a, b, c to include at least one non-zero digit (to form valid 6-digit numbers), but the Lean code does not enforce this.\n   - All other components (distinct digits, remainder calculation, maximality condition) align correctly.\n\n4. Conclusion: The Lean statement is **Incorrect** due to invalid number inclusion in the sum.\n\n5. Accuracy Confirmation:\n   - **Mismatch 1**: The Lean code includes 6-digit sequences starting with 0 (e.g., if a=0, first digit could be 0), which are not valid 6-digit numbers. This inflates the sum and violates the problem's constraints.\n   - **Mismatch 2**: No explicit constraint ensures at least one of a, b, c is non-zero, though this is implied by the problem's requirement for 6-digit numbers.\n\nResult: Incorrect\n\nTherefore, the outcome of the consistency verification is: Incorrect\n\n</round>\n\n# Round 2 (Autoformalization)\n\n<round>\n\nLooking at this problem, I need to formalize the statement about 6-digit numbers formed from three distinct digits, where the sum equals a specific value, and we want to maximize the remainder when the 3-digit number abc is divided by 100.\n\nKey considerations:\n1. The digits a, b, c must be distinct and from 0-9\n2. 6-digit numbers cannot start with 0 (so the first digit must be non-zero)\n3. We need to sum all valid 6-digit numbers formed using only a, b, c\n4. Find the maximum remainder when abc (as a 3-digit number) is divided by 100\n\nLet me search for relevant Mathlib concepts to properly formalize this:\n\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"leansearch\", \"arguments\": {\"query\": \"Finset sum over functions from Fin n to finite set\", \"num_results\": 8}}\n</tool_call>\n<tool_response>\n[1] Finset.sum_range  (theorem)\n    ∀ {M : Type u_2} [inst : AddCommMonoid M] {n : ℕ} (f : ℕ → M), ∑ i ∈ Finset.range n, f i = ∑ i, f ↑i\n    For any additive commutative monoid $ M $, natural number $ n $, and function $ f : \\mathbb{N} \\to M $, the sum of $ f(i) $ for $ i $ in the finite set $ \\{0, 1, \\ldots, n - 1\\} $ is equal to the sum of $ f(i) $ for all $ i $ in the finite type $ \\mathrm{Fin}(n) $, where each element $ i \\in \\mathrm{Fin}(n) $ is identified with its corresponding natural number.\n[2] List.sum_ofFn  (theorem)\n    ∀ {M : Type u_2} [inst : AddCommMonoid M] {n : ℕ} {f : Fin n → M}, (List.ofFn f).sum = ∑ i, f i\n    Given an additive commutative monoid $M$ and a function $f : \\mathrm{Fin}(n) \\to M$, the sum of $f$ over the universal finite set $\\mathrm{Fin}(n)$ is equal to the summation of $f(i)$ for all $i \\in \\mathrm{Fin}(n)$. Formally, $\\sum_{i \\in \\mathrm{Fin}(n)} f(i) = \\sum_{k=0}^{n-1} f(k)$.\n[3] List.sum_take_ofFn  (theorem)\n    ∀ {M : Type u_2} [inst : AddCommMonoid M] {n : ℕ} (f : Fin n → M) (i : ℕ),\n  (List.take i (List.ofFn f)).sum = ∑ j with ↑j < i, f j\n    For any additive commutative monoid $M$ and any function $f : \\mathrm{Fin}(n) \\to M$, the sum of $f(i)$ over the universal finite set $\\mathrm{Fin}(n)$ is equal to the formal sum $\\sum_{i=0}^{n-1} f(i)$.\n[4] finsum_mem_eq_toFinset_sum  (theorem)\n    ∀ {α : Type u_1} {M : Type u_5} [inst : AddCommMonoid M] (f : α → M) (s : Set α) [inst_1 : Fintype ↑s],\n  ∑ᶠ (i : α) (_ : i ∈ s), f i = ∑ i ∈ s.toFinset, f i\n    For any function $ f : \\alpha \\to M $ where $ M $ is an additive commutative monoid, and for any finite set $ s \\subseteq \\alpha $, the finsum of $ f $ over $ s $ is equal to the sum of $ f $ over the corresponding Finset $ s.toFinset $. This equality holds under the assumption that $ \\alpha $ is equipped with a finite type instance for $ s $.\n[5] Fin.sum_ofFn  (theorem)\n    ∀ {M : Type u_2} [inst : AddCommMonoid M] {n : ℕ} (f : Fin n → M), (List.ofFn f).sum = ∑ i, f i\n    For any additive commutative monoid $ M $ and natural number $ n $, and for any function $ f : \\mathrm{Fin}(n) \\to M $, the sum of the list constructed by applying $ f $ to each element of $ \\mathrm{Fin}(n) $ via `List.ofFn` is equal to the sum of $ f(i) $ for all $ i \\in \\mathrm{Fin}(n) $. That is, $ \\left(\\mathrm{List.ofFn}(f)\\right).\\mathrm{sum} = \\sum_{i \\in \\mathrm{Fin}(n)} f(i) $.\n[6] Finset.sum_apply'  (theorem)\n    : (∑ k ∈ s, f k) i = ∑ k ∈ s, f k i\n    For any finite set $ s $ and any function $ f $ mapping elements of $ s $ to finite support functions $ \\iota \\to_0 M $, the evaluation of the sum $ \\sum_{k \\in s} f(k) $ at a point $ i $ is equal to the sum $ \\sum_{k \\in s} f(k)(i) $.\n[7] Finset.sum_set_coe  (theorem)\n    ∀ {ι : Type u_1} {M : Type u_4} [inst : AddCommMonoid M] {f : ι → M} (s : Set ι) [inst_1 : Fintype ↑s],\n  ∑ i, f ↑i = ∑ i ∈ s.toFinset, f i\n    For any additive commutative monoid $ M $, type $ \\iota $, function $ f : \\iota \\to M $, and finite set $ s \\subseteq \\iota $, the sum of $ f(i) $ over all $ i \\in s $ is equal to the sum of $ f(i) $ over the Finset representation of $ s $. Formally, $ \\sum_{i \\in s} f(i) = \\sum_{i \\in s.\\text{toFinset}} f(i) $.\n[8] Finsupp.finset_sum_apply  (theorem)\n    [AddCommMonoid N] (S : Finset ι) (f : ι → α →₀ N) (a : α) : (∑ i ∈ S, f i) a = ∑ i ∈ S, f i a\n    For any finite set $S$ of indices, a function $f$ mapping each $i \\in S$ to a finite support function $f_i : \\alpha \\to_0 N$, and for any $a \\in \\alpha$, the evaluation of the sum $\\sum_{i \\in S} f_i$ at $a$ equals $\\sum_{i \\in S} f_i(a)$. Here, $N$ is an additive commutative monoid.\n</tool_response>\nNow let me formalize the statement. The key insight is that we need to:\n1. Ensure a, b, c are distinct digits (0-9)\n2. Only count 6-digit numbers where the first digit is non-zero\n3. Sum all such valid numbers\n4. Find the maximum remainder when abc is divided by 100\n\n```lean4\nimport Mathlib\n\nopen Finset Nat\n\n/-- \nGiven three distinct digits a, b, c, consider all valid 6-digit numbers \nformed using only these digits (first digit cannot be 0).\nIf the sum of all such numbers equals 593999406, find the maximum remainder \nwhen the 3-digit number abc is divided by 100.\n-/\ntheorem digit_sum_remainder_problem : \n  ∃ (a b c : ℕ), \n    -- a, b, c are distinct digits\n    a < 10 ∧ b < 10 ∧ c < 10 ∧\n    a ≠ b ∧ b ≠ c ∧ a ≠ c ∧\n    -- At least one digit is non-zero (to form valid 6-digit numbers)\n    (a ≠ 0 ∨ b ≠ 0 ∨ c ≠ 0) ∧\n    -- The sum of all valid 6-digit numbers formed from {a, b, c} equals 593999406\n    (∑ f : Fin 6 → Fin 3, \n      let digit_val := fun (choice : Fin 3) => if choice = 0 then a else if choice = 1 then b else c\n      let first_digit := digit_val (f 0)\n      -- Only include numbers where first digit is non-zero\n      if first_digit ≠ 0 then\n        digit_val (f 0) * 100000 + digit_val (f 1) * 10000 + digit_val (f 2) * 1000 + \n        digit_val (f 3) * 100 + digit_val (f 4) * 10 + digit_val (f 5)\n      else 0) = 593999406 ∧\n    -- The maximum remainder when abc (as 3-digit number) is divided by 100\n    (∀ (a' b' c' : ℕ), \n      a' < 10 → b' < 10 → c' < 10 →\n      a' ≠ b' → b' ≠ c' → a' ≠ c' →\n      (a' ≠ 0 ∨ b' ≠ 0 ∨ c' ≠ 0) →\n      (∑ f : Fin 6 → Fin 3, \n        let digit_val := fun (choice : Fin 3) => if choice = 0 then a' else if choice = 1 then b' else c'\n        let first_digit := digit_val (f 0)\n        if first_digit ≠ 0 then\n          digit_val (f 0) * 100000 + digit_val (f 1) * 10000 + digit_val (f 2) * 1000 + \n          digit_val (f 3) * 100 + digit_val (f 4) * 10 + digit_val (f 5)\n        else 0) = 593999406 →\n      (100 * a' + 10 * b' + c') % 100 ≤ (100 * a + 10 * b + c) % 100) := by sorry\n```\n\n# Round 2 (Semantic Self-validation)\n\n1. Math Assertion Analysis: The problem requires finding the maximum remainder of the 3-digit number $abc$ (value $100a + 10b + c$) modulo 100. The constraints are: $a, b, c$ are distinct digits ($0-9$); the sum of all valid 6-digit numbers formed using only these digits is $593,999,406$; valid 6-digit numbers cannot start with 0.\n2. Lean Statement Analysis (Proof Ignored): The theorem asserts the existence of distinct digits $a, b, c$ satisfying the sum condition and maximizing the remainder. The sum is computed by iterating over all $3^6$ sequences of digits. Crucially, it includes `if first_digit ≠ 0 then ... else 0`, which correctly excludes numbers with leading zeros from the sum. It also ensures at least one digit is non-zero.\n3. Comparative Verification: The Lean statement correctly implements the \"no leading zero\" constraint for the 6-digit numbers, which was the primary failure in the previous version. The variables, distinctness, and sum value match the problem statement. The maximization of the remainder is correctly formalized. While the phrase \"three digit number abc\" implies $a \\neq 0$, the large sum value ($~5.9 \\times 10^8$) mathematically forces $a, b, c$ to be large non-zero digits, making an explicit $a \\neq 0$ constraint redundant for correctness.\n4. Conclusion: The Lean statement is a correct formalization.\n5. Accuracy Confirmation: The formal statement matches the natural language problem in all structural and semantic aspects, including the critical handling of valid 6-digit numbers.\n\nResult: Correct\n\nTherefore, the outcome of the consistency verification is: Correct\n\n</round>\n\n</think>\n\n<answer>\n\n```lean4\nimport Mathlib\n\nopen Finset Nat\n\n/-- \nGiven three distinct digits a, b, c, consider all valid 6-digit numbers \nformed using only these digits (first digit cannot be 0).\nIf the sum of all such numbers equals 593999406, find the maximum remainder \nwhen the 3-digit number abc is divided by 100.\n-/\ntheorem digit_sum_remainder_problem : \n  ∃ (a b c : ℕ), \n    -- a, b, c are distinct digits\n    a < 10 ∧ b < 10 ∧ c < 10 ∧\n    a ≠ b ∧ b ≠ c ∧ a ≠ c ∧\n    -- At least one digit is non-zero (to form valid 6-digit numbers)\n    (a ≠ 0 ∨ b ≠ 0 ∨ c ≠ 0) ∧\n    -- The sum of all valid 6-digit numbers formed from {a, b, c} equals 593999406\n    (∑ f : Fin 6 → Fin 3, \n      let digit_val := fun (choice : Fin 3) => if choice = 0 then a else if choice = 1 then b else c\n      let first_digit := digit_val (f 0)\n      -- Only include numbers where first digit is non-zero\n      if first_digit ≠ 0 then\n        digit_val (f 0) * 100000 + digit_val (f 1) * 10000 + digit_val (f 2) * 1000 + \n        digit_val (f 3) * 100 + digit_val (f 4) * 10 + digit_val (f 5)\n      else 0) = 593999406 ∧\n    -- The maximum remainder when abc (as 3-digit number) is divided by 100\n    (∀ (a' b' c' : ℕ), \n      a' < 10 → b' < 10 → c' < 10 →\n      a' ≠ b' → b' ≠ c' → a' ≠ c' →\n      (a' ≠ 0 ∨ b' ≠ 0 ∨ c' ≠ 0) →\n      (∑ f : Fin 6 → Fin 3, \n        let digit_val := fun (choice : Fin 3) => if choice = 0 then a' else if choice = 1 then b' else c'\n        let first_digit := digit_val (f 0)\n        if first_digit ≠ 0 then\n          digit_val (f 0) * 100000 + digit_val (f 1) * 10000 + digit_val (f 2) * 1000 + \n          digit_val (f 3) * 100 + digit_val (f 4) * 10 + digit_val (f 5)\n        else 0) = 593999406 →\n      (100 * a' + 10 * b' + c') % 100 ≤ (100 * a + 10 * b + c) % 100) := by sorry\n```\n\n</answer>"}], "informal_statement": "Consider the set of all 6-digit numbers consisting of only three digits, $a,b,c$ where $a,b,c$ are distinct. Suppose the sum of all these numbers is $593999406$. What is the largest remainder when the three digit number $abc$ is divided by $100$?", "num_rounds": 2, "num_tool_calls": 4, "tools_used": ["leansearch"], "success": true, "syntax_pass": true, "syntax_rewrites": [], "retrieval_domain": "math", "dataset_name": "numina15_aops_forum", "question_id": "aops_forum_203964"}
{"messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "Think step by step to translate the mathematical problem in natural language to Lean 4, and verify the consistency.\nIf $n$ is a multiple of three, what is the remainder when $(n + 4) + (n + 6) + (n + 8)$ is divided by $9$? Show that it is 0."}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "<think>\n# Round 1 (Autoformalization)\n\n<round>\n\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"leansearch\", \"arguments\": {\"query\": \"multiple of three divisibility 3 ∣ n notation\", \"num_results\": 8}}\n</tool_call>\n<tool_response>\n[1] Nat.three_dvd_iff  (theorem)\n    (n : ℕ) : 3 ∣ n ↔ 3 ∣ (digits 10 n).sum\n    For any natural number $n$, $3$ divides $n$ if and only if $3$ divides the sum of the digits of $n$ in base $10$.\n[2] Polynomial.Chebyshev.derivative_U_eval_one_dvd  (theorem)\n    (n : ℤ) : (3 : 𝔽) ∣ (n + 2) * (n + 1) * n\n    For any integer $ n $, the product $ n(n + 1)(n + 2) $ is divisible by 3.\n[3] Num.dvd_iff_mod_eq_zero  (theorem)\n    {m n : Num} : m ∣ n ↔ n % m = 0\n    For any binary natural numbers $m$ and $n$, $m$ divides $n$ if and only if the remainder when $n$ is divided by $m$ is zero, i.e., $n \\bmod m = 0$.\n[4] Nat.modEq_zero_iff_dvd  (theorem)\n    : a ≡ 0 [MOD n] ↔ n ∣ a\n    For natural numbers $a$ and $n$, $a$ is congruent to $0$ modulo $n$ if and only if $n$ divides $a$.\n[5] PosNum.dvd  (instance)\n    : Dvd PosNum\n    For any two positive natural numbers $ m $ and $ n $, $ m $ divides $ n $ if there exists a positive natural number $ k $ such that $ n = m \\cdot k $.\n[6] Dvd.dvd.modEq_zero_nat  (theorem)\n    (h : n ∣ a) : a ≡ 0 [MOD n]\n    If $n$ divides $a$, then $a \\equiv 0 \\pmod{n}$.\n[7] Dioph.term_D∣_  (definition)\n    : Lean.TrailingParserDescr✝\n    The parser for the divisibility relation $D \\mid$ used in Diophantine equations, where $a \\mid b$ denotes that $a$ divides $b$.\n[8] semigroupDvd  (instance)\n    : Dvd α\n    For any semigroup $\\alpha$ with multiplication $\\cdot$, the divisibility relation $a \\mid b$ is defined by the existence of an element $c \\in \\alpha$ such that $b = a \\cdot c$.\n</tool_response>\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"leansearch\", \"arguments\": {\"query\": \"modular arithmetic remainder mod 9 Nat.mod\", \"num_results\": 8}}\n</tool_call>\n<tool_response>\n[1] Nat.mod_modEq  (theorem)\n    (a n) : a % n ≡ a [MOD n]\n    For any natural numbers $a$ and $n$, the remainder when $a$ is divided by $n$ is congruent to $a$ modulo $n$. In symbols, $a \\% n \\equiv a \\pmod{n}$.\n[2] Nat.modEq_nine_digits_sum  (theorem)\n    (n : ℕ) : n ≡ (digits 10 n).sum [MOD 9]\n    For any natural number $ n $, $ n $ is congruent modulo 9 to the sum of its decimal digits, i.e., $ n \\equiv \\sum (\\text{digits}_{10}(n)) \\pmod{9} $.\n[3] Nat.mod_eq_of_modEq  (theorem)\n    {a b n} (h : a ≡ b [MOD n]) (hb : b < n) : a % n = b\n    If $a \\equiv b \\pmod{n}$ and $b < n$, then $a \\bmod n = b$.\n[4] Nat.modEq_mul_modulus_add_iff  (theorem)\n    : a ≡ b * n + c [MOD n] ↔ a ≡ c [MOD n]\n    For any natural numbers $a, b, c, n$, the congruence $a \\equiv b \\cdot n + c \\pmod{n}$ holds if and only if $a \\equiv c \\pmod{n}$.\n[5] PNat.mod_add_div  (theorem)\n    (m k : ℕ+) : (mod m k + k * div m k : ℕ) = m\n    For any positive natural numbers $ m $ and $ k $, the remainder $ r $ when $ m $ is divided by $ k $ and the quotient $ q $ satisfy $ r + k \\cdot q = m $.\n[6] Mathlib.Meta.NormNum.isInt_emod  (theorem)\n    {a b q m a' : ℤ} {b' r : ℕ} (ha : IsInt a a') (hb : IsNat b b') (hm : q * b' = m) (h : r + m = a')\n  (h₂ : Nat.blt r b' = true) : IsNat (a % b) r\n    Let $a$ be an integer equal to $a'$, and $b$ be a natural number equal to $b'$. If there exist integers $q$ and $m$ such that $m = q \\cdot b'$ and $a' = r + m$, where $r$ is a natural number less than $b'$, then the remainder when $a$ is divided by $b$ is $r$.\n[7] AddCommGroup.modEq_iff_natModEq  (theorem)\n    {a b n : ℕ} : a ≡ b [PMOD n] ↔ a ≡ b [MOD n]\n    For any natural numbers $a$, $b$, and $n$, the existence of natural numbers $m$ and $k$ such that $m \\cdot n + a = k \\cdot n + b$ is equivalent to $a$ and $b$ having the same remainder when divided by $n$.\n[8] Nat.ModEq.refl  (theorem)\n    (a : ℕ) : a ≡ a [MOD n]\n    For any natural number $a$, $a \\equiv a \\pmod{n}$.\n</tool_response>\nNow I have enough information to formalize the statement. The problem asks:\n- Given that $n$ is a multiple of 3 (i.e., $3 \\mid n$)\n- Show that $(n + 4) + (n + 6) + (n + 8)$ divided by 9 has remainder 0\n\nLet me simplify: $(n + 4) + (n + 6) + (n + 8) = 3n + 18$\n\nSince $3 \\mid n$, we have $n = 3k$ for some $k$, so $3n + 18 = 3(3k) + 18 = 9k + 18 = 9(k + 2)$, which is clearly divisible by 9.\n\nThe formal statement should express that if $3 \\mid n$, then $(n + 4) + (n + 6) + (n + 8) \\equiv 0 \\pmod{9}$, or equivalently, $((n + 4) + (n + 6) + (n + 8)) \\% 9 = 0$.\n\n```lean4\nimport Mathlib\n\ntheorem remainder_sum_divisible_by_nine (n : ℕ) (h : 3 ∣ n) : ((n + 4) + (n + 6) + (n + 8)) % 9 = 0 := by sorry\n```\n\n# Round 1 (Semantic Self-validation)\n\nYour brief analysis:\n1. Math Assertion Analysis: The problem states that if n is a multiple of 3, then the remainder when (n+4)+(n+6)+(n+8) is divided by 9 is 0. Key components: n ∈ ℕ, 3 ∣ n, expression (n+4)+(n+6)+(n+8), modulo 9 operation, conclusion that the result is 0.\n2. Lean Statement Analysis (Proof Ignored): The theorem uses n : ℕ and h : 3 ∣ n, matching the problem's constraints. The conclusion ((n + 4) + (n + 6) + (n + 8)) % 9 = 0 directly corresponds to the problem's requirement to show the remainder is 0.\n3. Comparative Verification: The Lean statement matches the math problem in variables (n ∈ ℕ), hypothesis (3 ∣ n), expression ((n+4)+(n+6)+(n+8)), and conclusion (mod 9 equals 0). All constraints and logic structure are preserved.\n4. Conclusion: The Lean statement correctly formalizes the problem's requirements without omissions or additions.\n5. Accuracy Confirmation: The Lean statement matches the mathematical problem in all structural and semantic components. The variables, hypothesis, expression, and conclusion are all correctly formalized.\n\nResult: Correct\n\nTherefore, the outcome of the consistency verification is: Correct\n\n</round>\n\n</think>\n\n<answer>\n\n```lean4\nimport Mathlib\n\ntheorem remainder_sum_divisible_by_nine (n : ℕ) (h : 3 ∣ n) : ((n + 4) + (n + 6) + (n + 8)) % 9 = 0 := by sorry\n```\n\n</answer>"}], "informal_statement": "If $n$ is a multiple of three, what is the remainder when $(n + 4) + (n + 6) + (n + 8)$ is divided by $9$? Show that it is 0.", "num_rounds": 1, "num_tool_calls": 2, "tools_used": ["leansearch"], "success": true, "syntax_pass": true, "syntax_rewrites": [], "retrieval_domain": "math", "dataset_name": "minif2f_test", "question_id": "mathd_numbertheory_582"}
{"messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "Think step by step to translate the mathematical problem in natural language to Lean 4, and verify the consistency.\nSuppose $[a \\,\\,\\, b]$ denotes the average of $a$ and $b$, and $\\{a\\,\\,\\,b\\,\\,\\,c\\}$ denotes the average of $a$, $b$, and $c$. What is $\\{\\{1\\,\\,\\, 1\\,\\,\\, 0\\}\\,\\,\\, [0\\,\\,\\, 1]\\,\\,\\, 0\\}$?\n\n$ \\textbf{(A)}\\ \\frac{2}{9} \\qquad\\textbf{(B)}\\ \\frac{5}{18} \\qquad\\textbf{(C)}\\ \\frac{1}{3} \\qquad\\textbf{(D)}\\ \\frac{7}{18} \\qquad\\textbf{(E)}\\ \\frac{2}{3} $"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "<think>\n# Round 1 (Autoformalization)\n\n<round>\n\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"leansearch\", \"arguments\": {\"query\": \"average of two numbers mean arithmetic mean\", \"num_results\": 8}}\n</tool_call>\n<tool_response>\n[1] add_div_two_lt_right  (theorem)\n    : (a + b) / 2 < b ↔ a < b\n    For any elements $a$ and $b$, the inequality $\\frac{a + b}{2} < b$ holds if and only if $a < b$.\n[2] left_lt_add_div_two  (theorem)\n    : a < (a + b) / 2 ↔ a < b\n    For any elements $a$ and $b$, the inequality $a < \\frac{a + b}{2}$ holds if and only if $a < b$.\n[3] midpoint_eq_smul_add  (theorem)\n    (x y : V) : midpoint R x y = (⅟2 : R) • (x + y)\n    For any vectors $x$ and $y$ in the vector space $V$ over a ring $R$, the midpoint between $x$ and $y$ is equal to $\\frac{1}{2} \\cdot (x + y)$, where $\\frac{1}{2}$ denotes the multiplicative inverse of $2$ in $R$.\n[4] Finset.centroid_pair_fin  (theorem)\n    [Invertible (2 : k)] (p : Fin 2 → P) : univ.centroid k p = (2⁻¹ : k) • (p 1 -ᵥ p 0) +ᵥ p 0\n    For any two points $p_0$ and $p_1$ in an affine space over a division ring $k$ where 2 is invertible, the centroid (midpoint) of the pair is given by $\\frac{1}{2} \\cdot (p_1 - p_0) + p_0$, where $\\cdot$ denotes scalar multiplication and $-$ denotes vector subtraction in the affine space structure.\n[5] sup_eq_half_smul_add_add_abs_sub'  (theorem)\n    (x y : M) : x ⊔ y = (2⁻¹ : 𝕜) • (x + y + |y - x|)\n    For any elements $x$ and $y$ in $M$, the supremum $x \\sqcup y$ is equal to $\\frac{1}{2} \\cdot (x + y + |y - x|)$, where the absolute value $|y - x|$ is defined as the supremum of $y - x$ and $-(y - x)$.\n[6] interval_average_eq_div  (theorem)\n    (f : ℝ → ℝ) (a b : ℝ) : (⨍ x in a..b, f x) = (∫ x in a..b, f x) / (b - a)\n    For any real-valued function $ f : \\mathbb{R} \\to \\mathbb{R} $ and real numbers $ a, b $ with $ a < b $, the average value of $ f $ over the interval $[a, b]$ satisfies $ \\text{⨍}_{a}^{b} f(x) \\, dx = \\frac{1}{b - a} \\int_{a}^{b} f(x) \\, dx $. This equates the interval average integral notation $ \\text{⨍} $ to the standard integral normalized by the interval length $ b - a $.\n[7] midpoint  (definition)\n    (x y : P) : P\n    The midpoint between two points $x$ and $y$ in an affine space $P$ over a ring $R$ is defined as the affine combination $\\text{lineMap}(x, y, \\frac{1}{2})$, which represents the point halfway along the line segment connecting $x$ and $y$ using scalar $\\frac{1}{2}$ from the ring $R$.\n[8] MeasureTheory.average_add_measure  (theorem)\n    [IsFiniteMeasure μ] {ν : Measure α} [IsFiniteMeasure ν] {f : α → E} (hμ : Integrable f μ) (hν : Integrable f ν) :\n  ⨍ x, f x ∂(μ + ν) =\n    (μ.real univ / (μ.real univ + ν.real univ)) • ⨍ x, f x ∂μ +\n      (ν.real univ / (μ.real univ + ν.real univ)) • ⨍ x, f x ∂ν\n    For finite measures $\\mu$ and $\\nu$ on a measurable space $\\alpha$, and an integrable function $f$ with respect to both measures, the average of $f$ with respect to the sum measure $\\mu + \\nu$ equals the weighted sum of the averages of $f$ over $\\mu$ and $\\nu$. Specifically, $$\\frac{1}{\\mu(\\alpha) + \\nu(\\alpha)} \\int_{\\alpha} f \\, d(\\mu + \\nu) = \\frac{\\mu(\\alpha)}{\\mu(\\alpha) + \\nu(\\alpha)} \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\mu(\\alpha)} \\int_{\\alpha} f \\, d\\mu + \\frac{\\nu(\\alpha)}{\\mu(\\alpha) + \\nu(\\alpha)} \\cdot \\frac{1}{\\nu(\\alpha)} \\int_{\\alpha} f \\, d\\nu.$$\n</tool_response>\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"leansearch\", \"arguments\": {\"query\": \"rational numbers ℚ division field arithmetic\", \"num_results\": 5}}\n</tool_call>\n<tool_response>\n[1] Field.ratCast_def  (theorem)\n    ∀ {K : Type u} [self : Field K] (q : ℚ), ↑q = ↑q.num / ↑q.den\n    The canonical embedding of a rational number $ q $ into a field $ K $ is equal to the division of the numerator of $ q $ by the denominator of $ q $ in $ K $, i.e., $ \\frac{\\text{num}(q)}{\\text{den}(q)} $.\n[2] Rat.div_def'  (theorem)\n    (q r : ℚ) : q / r = (q.num * r.den) /. (q.den * r.num)\n    For any rational numbers $ q $ and $ r $, the division $ q / r $ is equal to $ \\frac{\\text{num}(q) \\cdot \\text{den}(r)}{\\text{den}(q) \\cdot \\text{num}(r)} $, where $ \\text{num} $ and $ \\text{den} $ denote the numerator and denominator of a rational number respectively.\n[3] Rat.instDivisionRing  (instance)\n    : DivisionRing ℚ\n    The rational numbers $\\mathbb{Q}$ form a division ring under the usual addition and multiplication operations, with $0$ as the additive identity, $1$ as the multiplicative identity, and every non-zero element having a multiplicative inverse.\n[4] Rat.num_div_den  (theorem)\n    (r : ℚ) : (r.num : ℚ) / (r.den : ℚ) = r\n    For any rational number $ r $, $ r $ is equal to the division of its numerator by its denominator, i.e., $ r = \\frac{r.\\text{num}}{r.\\text{den}} $.\n[5] Field.nnratCast_def  (theorem)\n    ∀ {K : Type u} [self : Field K] (q : ℚ≥0), ↑q = ↑q.num / ↑q.den\n    For any non-negative rational number $ q $ in a field $ K $, the canonical embedding of $ q $ into $ K $ is equal to the quotient of its numerator and denominator, i.e., $ \\frac{\\text{num}(q)}{\\text{den}(q)} $, where $ \\text{num}(q) $ and $ \\text{den}(q) $ are the numerator and denominator of $ q $ expressed in lowest terms as natural numbers.\n</tool_response>\nBased on the search results, I can see that Lean 4's Mathlib has support for rational numbers (ℚ) and division operations. The problem involves computing nested averages of rational numbers.\n\nLet me formalize this step by step:\n- Define $[a \\,\\,\\, b]$ as the average of two numbers: $(a + b) / 2$\n- Define $\\{a\\,\\,\\,b\\,\\,\\,c\\}$ as the average of three numbers: $(a + b + c) / 3$\n- State that the nested expression equals $7/18$\n\n```lean4\nimport Mathlib\n\n/-- \nThe problem defines two average operations:\n- [a b] = (a + b) / 2 (average of two numbers)\n- {a b c} = (a + b + c) / 3 (average of three numbers)\nThis theorem states that {{1 1 0} [0 1] 0} = 7/18.\n-/\ntheorem nested_average_computation : \n  let avg2 : ℚ → ℚ → ℚ := fun a b => (a + b) / 2\n  let avg3 : ℚ → ℚ → ℚ → ℚ := fun a b c => (a + b + c) / 3\n  avg3 (avg3 1 1 0) (avg2 0 1) 0 = 7 / 18 := by sorry\n```\n\n# Round 1 (Semantic Self-validation)\n\nYour brief analysis:\n1. Math Assertion Analysis: The problem defines two operations: [a b] = (a + b)/2 and {a b c} = (a + b + c)/3. The task is to compute the nested expression {{1 1 0} [0 1] 0}. The expected result is one of the given options, with the Lean theorem claiming 7/18.\n2. Lean Statement Analysis (Proof Ignored): The Lean code defines avg2 and avg3 as functions over ℚ, correctly implementing the averages. The theorem states that avg3 (avg3 1 1 0) (avg2 0 1) 0 equals 7/18. All operations and nesting match the problem's structure.\n3. Comparative Verification: The Lean code precisely mirrors the natural language operations and nesting. The computation steps (inner averages first, then outer average) align exactly. The use of ℚ is appropriate for fractional results. No missing constraints or semantic drift detected.\n4. Conclusion: The Lean formalization is mathematically equivalent to the problem statement.\n5. Accuracy Confirmation: All components match perfectly. The nested structure, operations, and result (7/18) are correctly captured. The calculation steps confirm the result matches the problem's requirements.\n\nResult: Correct\n\nTherefore, the outcome of the consistency verification is: Correct\n\n</round>\n\n</think>\n\n<answer>\n\n```lean4\nimport Mathlib\n\n/-- \nThe problem defines two average operations:\n- [a b] = (a + b) / 2 (average of two numbers)\n- {a b c} = (a + b + c) / 3 (average of three numbers)\nThis theorem states that {{1 1 0} [0 1] 0} = 7/18.\n-/\ntheorem nested_average_computation : \n  let avg2 : ℚ → ℚ → ℚ := fun a b => (a + b) / 2\n  let avg3 : ℚ → ℚ → ℚ → ℚ := fun a b c => (a + b + c) / 3\n  avg3 (avg3 1 1 0) (avg2 0 1) 0 = 7 / 18 := by sorry\n```\n\n</answer>"}], "informal_statement": "Suppose $[a \\,\\,\\, b]$ denotes the average of $a$ and $b$, and $\\{a\\,\\,\\,b\\,\\,\\,c\\}$ denotes the average of $a$, $b$, and $c$. What is $\\{\\{1\\,\\,\\, 1\\,\\,\\, 0\\}\\,\\,\\, [0\\,\\,\\, 1]\\,\\,\\, 0\\}$?\n\n$ \\textbf{(A)}\\ \\frac{2}{9} \\qquad\\textbf{(B)}\\ \\frac{5}{18} \\qquad\\textbf{(C)}\\ \\frac{1}{3} \\qquad\\textbf{(D)}\\ \\frac{7}{18} \\qquad\\textbf{(E)}\\ \\frac{2}{3} $", "num_rounds": 1, "num_tool_calls": 2, "tools_used": ["leansearch"], "success": true, "syntax_pass": true, "syntax_rewrites": [], "retrieval_domain": "math", "dataset_name": "numina15_aops_forum", "question_id": "aops_forum_208268"}
{"messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "Think step by step to translate the mathematical problem in natural language to Lean 4, and verify the consistency.\n/-- Suppose that \\( f \\) is a function on the interval \\([1,3]\\) such that \\(-1 \\leq f(x) \\leq 1\\) for all \\( x \\) and \\( \\int_{1}^{3} f(x) \\, dx = 0 \\). How large can \\(\\int_{1}^{3} \\frac{f(x)}{x} \\, dx \\) be?-/"}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "<think>\n# Round 1 (Autoformalization)\n\n<round>\n\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"leansearch\", \"arguments\": {\"query\": \"integral on interval [a,b] bounded function supremum\", \"num_results\": 8}}\n</tool_call>\n<tool_response>\n[1] BoundedContinuousFunction.lintegral_le_edist_mul  (theorem)\n    (f : X →ᵇ ℝ≥0) (μ : Measure X) : (∫⁻ x, f x ∂μ) ≤ edist 0 f * (μ Set.univ)\n    For any bounded continuous function $ f : X \\to \\mathbb{R}_{\\geq 0} $ and any measure $ \\mu $ on $ X $, the Lebesgue integral of $ f $ with respect to $ \\mu $ satisfies $ \\int_X f \\, d\\mu \\leq \\|f\\|_\\infty \\cdot \\mu(X) $, where $ \\|f\\|_\\infty $ denotes the supremum norm of $ f $.\n[2] MeasureTheory.iSup₂_lintegral_le  (theorem)\n    {ι : Sort*} {ι' : ι → Sort*} (f : ∀ i, ι' i → α → ℝ≥0∞) : ⨆ (i) (j), ∫⁻ a, f i j a ∂μ ≤ ∫⁻ a, ⨆ (i) (j), f i j a ∂μ\n    For any index types $\\iota$ and $\\iota'$, and a non-negative measurable function $f : \\iota \\to (\\iota' i \\to \\alpha \\to [0, \\infty])$, the supremum over all $i$ and $j$ of the Lebesgue integral $\\int^{-} f_{i,j} \\, d\\mu$ is less than or equal to the Lebesgue integral of the supremum over all $i$ and $j$ of $f_{i,j}$, i.e., $\\bigvee_{i,j} \\int^{-} f_{i,j} \\, d\\mu \\leq \\int^{-} \\bigvee_{i,j} f_{i,j} \\, d\\mu$.\n[3] intervalIntegral.norm_integral_le_of_norm_le  (theorem)\n    {g : ℝ → ℝ} (hab : a ≤ b) (h : ∀ᵐ t ∂μ, t ∈ Set.Ioc a b → ‖f t‖ ≤ g t) (hbound : IntervalIntegrable g μ a b) :\n  ‖∫ t in a..b, f t ∂μ‖ ≤ ∫ t in a..b, g t ∂μ\n    Let `E` be a real Banach space, `f : ℝ → E` and `g : ℝ → ℝ` be two functions, and `μ` be a measure on `ℝ`. For any `a, b ∈ ℝ` with `a ≤ b`, if `g` is integrable on the interval from `a` to `b` and `‖f(t)‖ ≤ g(t)` for almost every `t` in the interval `(a, b]` with respect to `μ`, then the norm of the interval integral of `f` from `a` to `b` is bounded by the interval integral of `g` from `a` to `b`: $$ \\left\\|\\int_a^b f(t) \\, d\\mu\\right\\| \\le \\int_a^b g(t) \\, d\\mu $$\n[4] MeasureTheory.iSup_lintegral_le  (theorem)\n    {ι : Sort*} (f : ι → α → ℝ≥0∞) : ⨆ i, ∫⁻ a, f i a ∂μ ≤ ∫⁻ a, ⨆ i, f i a ∂μ\n    For any measure space $\\alpha$ with measure $\\mu$, and any family of non-negative measurable functions $f_i : \\alpha \\to [0, \\infty]$, the supremum of the Lebesgue integrals $\\int f_i \\, d\\mu$ is less than or equal to the Lebesgue integral of the pointwise supremum $\\sup_i f_i$, i.e., $\\sup_i \\int f_i \\, d\\mu \\leq \\int \\sup_i f_i \\, d\\mu$.\n[5] intervalIntegral.norm_integral_le_of_norm_le_const_ae  (theorem)\n    {a b C : ℝ} {f : ℝ → E} (h : ∀ᵐ x, x ∈ Ι a b → ‖f x‖ ≤ C) : ‖∫ x in a..b, f x‖ ≤ C * |b - a|\n    If the norm of a function $ f : \\mathbb{R} \\to E $ satisfies $\\|f(x)\\| \\leq C$ for almost every $x$ in the interval $[a, b]$, then the norm of its interval integral satisfies $\\left\\| \\int_a^b f(x) \\, dx \\right\\| \\leq C \\cdot |b - a|$. This holds for any normed space $E$ and real numbers $a, b$.\n[6] MeasureTheory.iSup_lintegral_measurable_le_eq_lintegral  (theorem)\n    (f : α → ℝ≥0∞) : ⨆ (g : α → ℝ≥0∞) (_ : Measurable g) (_ : g ≤ f), ∫⁻ a, g a ∂μ = ∫⁻ a, f a ∂μ\n    For a non-negative measurable function $ f : \\alpha \\to [0, \\infty] $, the Lebesgue integral $ \\int f \\, d\\mu $ is equal to the supremum of the integrals of all measurable functions $ g $ satisfying $ 0 \\leq g \\leq f $. This establishes that the integral of $ f $ can be represented as the least upper bound of integrals of measurable functions dominated by $ f $.\n[7] MeasureTheory.lintegral_le_iSup_mul  (theorem)\n    (f : α → ℝ≥0∞) : ∫⁻ x, f x ∂μ ≤ (⨆ x, f x) * μ .univ\n    For any non-negative measurable function $ f $ on a measure space $ (X, \\Sigma, \\mu) $, the Lebesgue integral of $ f $ satisfies $ \\int f \\, d\\mu \\leq \\left( \\sup_{x \\in X} f(x) \\right) \\cdot \\mu(X) $.\n[8] MeasureTheory.HasFiniteIntegral.of_mem_Icc_of_ne_top  (theorem)\n    [IsFiniteMeasure μ] {a b : ℝ≥0∞} (ha : a ≠ ⊤) (hb : b ≠ ⊤) {X : α → ℝ≥0∞} (h : ∀ᵐ ω ∂μ, X ω ∈ Set.Icc a b) :\n  HasFiniteIntegral X μ\n    Let $ \\mu $ be a finite measure on a measurable space $ \\alpha $, and let $ a, b \\in [0, \\infty) $ (i.e., $ a \\neq \\infty $, $ b \\neq \\infty $). If a function $ X : \\alpha \\to [0, \\infty] $ satisfies $ X(\\omega) \\in [a, b] $ for $ \\mu $-almost every $ \\omega \\in \\alpha $, then $ X $ has a finite integral with respect to $ \\mu $.\n</tool_response>\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"leansearch\", \"arguments\": {\"query\": \"IntervalIntegrable interval integral ∫ x in a..b\", \"num_results\": 8}}\n</tool_call>\n<tool_response>\n[1] intervalIntegral.intervalIntegrable_id  (theorem)\n    : IntervalIntegrable (fun x => x) μ a b\n    The identity function $ f(x) = x $ is integrable on the interval between $ a $ and $ b $ with respect to the measure $ \\mu $. This means $ f $ is integrable on both $ (a, b] $ and $ (b, a] $ under $ \\mu $.\n[2] IntervalIntegrable  (definition)\n    (f : ℝ → ε) (μ : Measure ℝ) (a b : ℝ) : Prop\n    The function $ f $ is integrable on the interval $ (a, b] $ and on the interval $ (b, a] $ with respect to the measure $ \\mu $.\n[3] IntervalIntegrable.def'  (theorem)\n    (h : IntervalIntegrable f μ a b) : IntegrableOn f (Ι a b) μ\n    If a function $ f $ is interval integrable with respect to measure $ \\mu $ on the interval between $ a $ and $ b $, then $ f $ is integrable on the interval $ (\\min(a, b), \\max(a, b)] $ with respect to $ \\mu $.\n[4] intervalIntegrable_iff_integrableOn_Ioc_of_le  (theorem)\n    (hab : a ≤ b) : IntervalIntegrable f μ a b ↔ IntegrableOn f (Ioc a b) μ\n    For real numbers $ a $ and $ b $ with $ a \\leq b $, a function $ f $ is interval integrable with respect to measure $ \\mu $ on the interval $ [a, b] $ if and only if $ f $ is integrable on the left-open right-closed interval $ (a, b] $ with respect to $ \\mu $. This equivalence holds under the condition that $ a $ is less than or equal to $ b $.\n[5] intervalIntegral.integral_eq_sub_of_hasDerivAt_of_le  (theorem)\n    (hab : a ≤ b) (hcont : ContinuousOn f (Icc a b)) (hderiv : ∀ x ∈ Ioo a b, HasDerivAt f (f' x) x)\n  (hint : IntervalIntegrable f' volume a b) : ∫ y in a..b, f' y = f b - f a\n    If $ f $ is continuous on the closed interval $[a, b]$, differentiable on the open interval $(a, b)$, and its derivative $ f' $ is integrable on $[a, b]$, then the integral of $ f' $ from $ a $ to $ b $ satisfies $\\int_a^b f'(y) \\, dy = f(b) - f(a)$.\n[6] intervalIntegral.integral_eq_sub_of_hasDerivAt_of_tendsto  (theorem)\n    (hab : a < b) {fa fb} (hderiv : ∀ x ∈ Ioo a b, HasDerivAt f (f' x) x) (hint : IntervalIntegrable f' volume a b)\n  (ha : Tendsto f (𝓝[>] a) (𝓝 fa)) (hb : Tendsto f (𝓝[<] b) (𝓝 fb)) : ∫ y in a..b, f' y = fb - fa\n    If $ f $ is differentiable on the open interval $ (a, b) $ with derivative $ f' $, $ f' $ is integrable on $ [a, b] $, and $ f $ approaches limits $ f(a) $ and $ f(b) $ at the endpoints, then the interval integral of $ f' $ from $ a $ to $ b $ equals $ f(b) - f(a) $. Formally, $\\int_a^b f'(x)\\,dx = f(b) - f(a)$.\n[7] intervalIntegral  (definition)\n    (f : ℝ → E) (a b : ℝ) (μ : Measure ℝ) : E\n    The interval integral of a function $ f : \\mathbb{R} \\to E $ from $ a $ to $ b $ with respect to the measure $ \\mu $ is defined as the difference between the Bochner integral of $ f $ over the left-open right-closed interval $ (a, b] $ and the Bochner integral of $ f $ over the interval $ (b, a] $.\n[8] MeasureTheory.Integrable.intervalIntegrable  (theorem)\n    (hf : Integrable f μ) : IntervalIntegrable f μ a b\n    If a function $ f $ is integrable with respect to a measure $ \\mu $, then $ f $ is integrable on the intervals $ (a, b] $ and $ (b, a] $ with respect to $ \\mu $.\n</tool_response>\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"leansearch\", \"arguments\": {\"query\": \"supremum of integral over functions with constraints\", \"num_results\": 8}}\n</tool_call>\n<tool_response>\n[1] MeasureTheory.iSup_lintegral_measurable_le_eq_lintegral  (theorem)\n    (f : α → ℝ≥0∞) : ⨆ (g : α → ℝ≥0∞) (_ : Measurable g) (_ : g ≤ f), ∫⁻ a, g a ∂μ = ∫⁻ a, f a ∂μ\n    For a non-negative measurable function $ f : \\alpha \\to [0, \\infty] $, the Lebesgue integral $ \\int f \\, d\\mu $ is equal to the supremum of the integrals of all measurable functions $ g $ satisfying $ 0 \\leq g \\leq f $. This establishes that the integral of $ f $ can be represented as the least upper bound of integrals of measurable functions dominated by $ f $.\n[2] MeasureTheory.iSup_lintegral_le  (theorem)\n    {ι : Sort*} (f : ι → α → ℝ≥0∞) : ⨆ i, ∫⁻ a, f i a ∂μ ≤ ∫⁻ a, ⨆ i, f i a ∂μ\n    For any measure space $\\alpha$ with measure $\\mu$, and any family of non-negative measurable functions $f_i : \\alpha \\to [0, \\infty]$, the supremum of the Lebesgue integrals $\\int f_i \\, d\\mu$ is less than or equal to the Lebesgue integral of the pointwise supremum $\\sup_i f_i$, i.e., $\\sup_i \\int f_i \\, d\\mu \\leq \\int \\sup_i f_i \\, d\\mu$.\n[3] MeasureTheory.iSup₂_lintegral_le  (theorem)\n    {ι : Sort*} {ι' : ι → Sort*} (f : ∀ i, ι' i → α → ℝ≥0∞) : ⨆ (i) (j), ∫⁻ a, f i j a ∂μ ≤ ∫⁻ a, ⨆ (i) (j), f i j a ∂μ\n    For any index types $\\iota$ and $\\iota'$, and a non-negative measurable function $f : \\iota \\to (\\iota' i \\to \\alpha \\to [0, \\infty])$, the supremum over all $i$ and $j$ of the Lebesgue integral $\\int^{-} f_{i,j} \\, d\\mu$ is less than or equal to the Lebesgue integral of the supremum over all $i$ and $j$ of $f_{i,j}$, i.e., $\\bigvee_{i,j} \\int^{-} f_{i,j} \\, d\\mu \\leq \\int^{-} \\bigvee_{i,j} f_{i,j} \\, d\\mu$.\n[4] MeasureTheory.lintegral_eq_nnreal  (theorem)\n    {m : MeasurableSpace α} (f : α → ℝ≥0∞) (μ : Measure α) :\n  ∫⁻ a, f a ∂μ = ⨆ (φ : α →ₛ ℝ≥0) (_ : ∀ x, ↑(φ x) ≤ f x), (φ.map ((↑) : ℝ≥0 → ℝ≥0∞)).lintegral μ\n    For a measure space $\\alpha$ with $\\sigma$-algebra $\\Sigma$, a measure $\\mu$, and a non-negative measurable function $f : \\alpha \\to [0, \\infty]$, the Lebesgue integral $\\int f \\, d\\mu$ equals the supremum of integrals of all simple functions $\\phi : \\alpha \\to_\\mathsf{s} \\mathbb{R}_{\\ge 0}$ satisfying $\\phi(x) \\le f(x)$ for all $x$. This expresses the Lebesgue integral as the least upper bound of integrals of approximating simple functions with values in the non-negative reals.\n[5] MeasureTheory.lintegral_iSup'  (theorem)\n    {f : ℕ → α → ℝ≥0∞} (hf : ∀ n, AEMeasurable (f n) μ) (h_mono : ∀ᵐ x ∂μ, Monotone fun n => f n x) :\n  ∫⁻ a, ⨆ n, f n a ∂μ = ⨆ n, ∫⁻ a, f n a ∂μ\n    For a sequence of non-negative measurable functions $\\{f_n\\}$ that are almost everywhere measurable with respect to a measure $\\mu$, and which is monotonically increasing almost everywhere, the Lebesgue integral of the supremum $\\sup_n f_n$ equals the supremum of the integrals $\\sup_n \\int f_n \\, d\\mu$.\n[6] MeasureTheory.setLIntegral_le_iSup_mul  (theorem)\n    (f : α → ℝ≥0∞) {s : Set α} (hs : MeasurableSet s) : ∫⁻ x in s, f x ∂μ ≤ (⨆ x ∈ s, f x) * μ s\n    For any non-negative measurable function $ f : \\alpha \\to [0, \\infty] $ and any measurable set $ s \\subseteq \\alpha $, the Lebesgue integral of $ f $ over $ s $ satisfies $ \\int_s f \\, d\\mu \\leq \\left( \\sup_{x \\in s} f(x) \\right) \\cdot \\mu(s) $.\n[7] MeasureTheory.lintegral_iSup_directed  (theorem)\n    [Countable β] {f : β → α → ℝ≥0∞} (hf : ∀ b, AEMeasurable (f b) μ) (h_directed : Directed (· ≤ ·) f) :\n  ∫⁻ a, ⨆ b, f b a ∂μ = ⨆ b, ∫⁻ a, f b a ∂μ\n    For a countable directed family of almost everywhere measurable functions $ f_b : \\alpha \\to [0, \\infty] $, the Lebesgue integral of their pointwise supremum equals the supremum of their individual integrals: $ \\int \\!\\!\\!\\!\\sup_{b} f_b(a) \\, d\\mu = \\sup_{b} \\int f_b(a) \\, d\\mu $. The directedness condition ensures that for any pair of indices $ b_1, b_2 $, there exists an index $ b_3 $ such that $ f_{b_3}(a) \\ge f_{b_1}(a) $ and $ f_{b_3}(a) \\ge f_{b_2}(a) $ for all $ a $.\n[8] MeasureTheory.SimpleFunc.le_sup_lintegral  (theorem)\n    (f g : α →ₛ ℝ≥0∞) : f.lintegral μ ⊔ g.lintegral μ ≤ (f ⊔ g).lintegral μ\n    For any two simple functions $ f $ and $ g $ from a measurable space $ \\alpha $ to the extended non-negative real numbers, the integral of their pointwise supremum with respect to a measure $ \\mu $ is greater than or equal to the maximum of their individual integrals. That is, $ \\int (f \\vee g) \\, d\\mu \\geq \\max\\left( \\int f \\, d\\mu, \\int g \\, d\\mu \\right) $.\n</tool_response>\nNow I have enough information to formalize the problem. The problem asks for the supremum of the integral ∫₁³ f(x)/x dx over all functions f satisfying:\n1. f is defined on [1,3]\n2. -1 ≤ f(x) ≤ 1 for all x ∈ [1,3]\n3. ∫₁³ f(x) dx = 0\n\nLet me write the Lean 4 formalization:\n\n```lean4\nimport Mathlib\n\nopen MeasureTheory Set\n\n/-- \nSuppose that f is a function on the interval [1,3] such that -1 ≤ f(x) ≤ 1 for all x \nand ∫₁³ f(x) dx = 0. The supremum of ∫₁³ f(x)/x dx over all such functions equals \nlog(3) - log(2) + log(1) - log(3/2) = 2*log(3/2) - log(2) = log(9/8).\nMore precisely, the supremum equals ∫₁² (-1)/x dx + ∫₂³ (1)/x dx = log(3) - 2*log(2) + log(3) = 2*log(3) - 2*log(2).\nActually, the optimal value is achieved by f(x) = -1 on [1,2] and f(x) = 1 on [2,3],\ngiving ∫₁³ f(x)/x dx = ∫₁² (-1)/x dx + ∫₂³ (1)/x dx = -log(2) + log(3) - log(2) = log(3) - 2*log(2) = log(3/4).\nWait, let me recalculate: ∫₁² (-1)/x dx = -[log(x)]₁² = -(log(2) - log(1)) = -log(2)\nAnd ∫₂³ (1)/x dx = [log(x)]₂³ = log(3) - log(2)\nSo total = -log(2) + log(3) - log(2) = log(3) - 2*log(2) = log(3) - log(4) = log(3/4).\n\nBut we need the constraint ∫₁³ f(x) dx = 0, which means ∫₁² (-1) dx + ∫₂³ (1) dx = -1 + 1 = 0. ✓\n\nThe supremum is log(3/4). But actually we want to maximize, so we want f(x) = 1 on [1,a] and f(x) = -1 on [a,3]\nfor some a where the integral constraint is satisfied: (a-1) - (3-a) = 0, so 2a = 4, a = 2.\nThen ∫₁³ f(x)/x dx = ∫₁² (1)/x dx + ∫₂³ (-1)/x dx = log(2) - (log(3) - log(2)) = 2*log(2) - log(3) = log(4/3).\n-/\ntheorem supremum_integral_bounded_zero_mean :\n  sSup {I : ℝ | ∃ f : ℝ → ℝ, \n    (∀ x ∈ Icc (1 : ℝ) 3, -1 ≤ f x ∧ f x ≤ 1) ∧\n    IntervalIntegrable f volume 1 3 ∧\n    (∫ x in (1 : ℝ)..(3 : ℝ), f x) = 0 ∧\n    IntervalIntegrable (fun x => f x / x) volume 1 3 ∧\n    I = ∫ x in (1 : ℝ)..(3 : ℝ), f x / x} = Real.log (4 / 3) := by sorry\n```\n\n[syntax_rewrite] unicode subscript digits → ASCII\n```lean4\nimport Mathlib\n\nopen MeasureTheory Set\n\n/-- \nSuppose that f is a function on the interval [1,3] such that -1 ≤ f(x) ≤ 1 for all x \nand ∫1³ f(x) dx = 0. The supremum of ∫1³ f(x)/x dx over all such functions equals \nlog(3) - log(2) + log(1) - log(3/2) = 2*log(3/2) - log(2) = log(9/8).\nMore precisely, the supremum equals ∫1² (-1)/x dx + ∫2³ (1)/x dx = log(3) - 2*log(2) + log(3) = 2*log(3) - 2*log(2).\nActually, the optimal value is achieved by f(x) = -1 on [1,2] and f(x) = 1 on [2,3],\ngiving ∫1³ f(x)/x dx = ∫1² (-1)/x dx + ∫2³ (1)/x dx = -log(2) + log(3) - log(2) = log(3) - 2*log(2) = log(3/4).\nWait, let me recalculate: ∫1² (-1)/x dx = -[log(x)]1² = -(log(2) - log(1)) = -log(2)\nAnd ∫2³ (1)/x dx = [log(x)]2³ = log(3) - log(2)\nSo total = -log(2) + log(3) - log(2) = log(3) - 2*log(2) = log(3) - log(4) = log(3/4).\n\nBut we need the constraint ∫1³ f(x) dx = 0, which means ∫1² (-1) dx + ∫2³ (1) dx = -1 + 1 = 0. ✓\n\nThe supremum is log(3/4). But actually we want to maximize, so we want f(x) = 1 on [1,a] and f(x) = -1 on [a,3]\nfor some a where the integral constraint is satisfied: (a-1) - (3-a) = 0, so 2a = 4, a = 2.\nThen ∫1³ f(x)/x dx = ∫1² (1)/x dx + ∫2³ (-1)/x dx = log(2) - (log(3) - log(2)) = 2*log(2) - log(3) = log(4/3).\n-/\ntheorem supremum_integral_bounded_zero_mean :\n  sSup {I : ℝ | ∃ f : ℝ → ℝ, \n    (∀ x ∈ Icc (1 : ℝ) 3, -1 ≤ f x ∧ f x ≤ 1) ∧\n    IntervalIntegrable f volume 1 3 ∧\n    (∫ x in (1 : ℝ)..(3 : ℝ), f x) = 0 ∧\n    IntervalIntegrable (fun x => f x / x) volume 1 3 ∧\n    I = ∫ x in (1 : ℝ)..(3 : ℝ), f x / x} = Real.log (4 / 3) := by sorry\n```\n\n# Round 1 (Semantic Self-validation)\n\nYour brief analysis:\n1. Math Assertion Analysis: The problem involves a function f on [1,3] with bounds -1 ≤ f(x) ≤ 1, integral ∫₁³ f(x)dx = 0, and asks for the maximum value of ∫₁³ f(x)/x dx. The key components are the function's domain, bounds, integral constraint, and the target integral's supremum.\n2. Lean Statement Analysis (Proof Ignored): The theorem defines the supremum of the set of integrals ∫₁³ f(x)/x dx for functions f satisfying: (a) f: ℝ→ℝ with -1 ≤ f(x) ≤ 1 on [1,3], (b) f is interval integrable on [1,3], (c) ∫₁³ f(x)dx = 0, (d) f(x)/x is interval integrable on [1,3]. The conclusion claims the supremum equals log(4/3).\n3. Comparative Verification: The Lean statement correctly captures all constraints (bounds, integral zero, integrability) and the target integral. The function's domain as ℝ→ℝ is acceptable since the problem only constrains f on [1,3]. The integrability conditions are necessary for the integral to be defined. The supremum matches the problem's request for the maximum value.\n4. Conclusion: The Lean statement accurately formalizes the mathematical problem.\n5. Accuracy Confirmation: All components match. The function's domain, bounds, integral constraints, and target integral are correctly captured. The use of supremum aligns with the problem's \"how large can... be?\" phrasing. Integrability conditions are appropriately added to ensure the integral is well-defined.\n\nResult: Correct\n\nTherefore, the outcome of the consistency verification is: Correct\n\n</round>\n\n</think>\n\n<answer>\n\n```lean4\nimport Mathlib\n\nopen MeasureTheory Set\n\n/-- \nSuppose that f is a function on the interval [1,3] such that -1 ≤ f(x) ≤ 1 for all x \nand ∫1³ f(x) dx = 0. The supremum of ∫1³ f(x)/x dx over all such functions equals \nlog(3) - log(2) + log(1) - log(3/2) = 2*log(3/2) - log(2) = log(9/8).\nMore precisely, the supremum equals ∫1² (-1)/x dx + ∫2³ (1)/x dx = log(3) - 2*log(2) + log(3) = 2*log(3) - 2*log(2).\nActually, the optimal value is achieved by f(x) = -1 on [1,2] and f(x) = 1 on [2,3],\ngiving ∫1³ f(x)/x dx = ∫1² (-1)/x dx + ∫2³ (1)/x dx = -log(2) + log(3) - log(2) = log(3) - 2*log(2) = log(3/4).\nWait, let me recalculate: ∫1² (-1)/x dx = -[log(x)]1² = -(log(2) - log(1)) = -log(2)\nAnd ∫2³ (1)/x dx = [log(x)]2³ = log(3) - log(2)\nSo total = -log(2) + log(3) - log(2) = log(3) - 2*log(2) = log(3) - log(4) = log(3/4).\n\nBut we need the constraint ∫1³ f(x) dx = 0, which means ∫1² (-1) dx + ∫2³ (1) dx = -1 + 1 = 0. ✓\n\nThe supremum is log(3/4). But actually we want to maximize, so we want f(x) = 1 on [1,a] and f(x) = -1 on [a,3]\nfor some a where the integral constraint is satisfied: (a-1) - (3-a) = 0, so 2a = 4, a = 2.\nThen ∫1³ f(x)/x dx = ∫1² (1)/x dx + ∫2³ (-1)/x dx = log(2) - (log(3) - log(2)) = 2*log(2) - log(3) = log(4/3).\n-/\ntheorem supremum_integral_bounded_zero_mean :\n  sSup {I : ℝ | ∃ f : ℝ → ℝ, \n    (∀ x ∈ Icc (1 : ℝ) 3, -1 ≤ f x ∧ f x ≤ 1) ∧\n    IntervalIntegrable f volume 1 3 ∧\n    (∫ x in (1 : ℝ)..(3 : ℝ), f x) = 0 ∧\n    IntervalIntegrable (fun x => f x / x) volume 1 3 ∧\n    I = ∫ x in (1 : ℝ)..(3 : ℝ), f x / x} = Real.log (4 / 3) := by sorry\n```\n\n</answer>"}], "informal_statement": "/-- Suppose that \\( f \\) is a function on the interval \\([1,3]\\) such that \\(-1 \\leq f(x) \\leq 1\\) for all \\( x \\) and \\( \\int_{1}^{3} f(x) \\, dx = 0 \\). How large can \\(\\int_{1}^{3} \\frac{f(x)}{x} \\, dx \\) be?-/", "num_rounds": 1, "num_tool_calls": 3, "tools_used": ["le