{"messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "Think step by step to translate the mathematical problem in natural language to Lean 4, and verify the consistency.\n1. (BUL 1) A pentagon $A B C D E$ inscribed in a circle for which $B C C S$.\n\nNote: The text \"$B C C S$\" seems to be a typo or an error in the original text, as it does not form a meaningful statement in the context of the problem. If you have more context or if this is part of a larger problem, please provide additional details."}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "<think>\n# Round 1 (Autoformalization)\n\n<round>\n\n<tool_call>\n{\"name\": \"leansearch\", \"arguments\": {\"query\": \"pentagon inscribed in circle cyclic polygon geometry\", \"num_results\": 8}}\n</tool_call>\n<tool_response>\n[1] EuclideanGeometry.Concyclic  (inductive)\n    {V : Type u_1} →\n  {P : Type u_2} →\n    [inst : NormedAddCommGroup V] → [NormedSpace ℝ V] → [inst_2 : MetricSpace P] → [NormedAddTorsor V P] → Set P → Prop\n    A set of points in P is concyclic if there exists a circle in the Euclidean space containing all the points.\n[2] EuclideanGeometry.Concyclic.subset  (theorem)\n    {ps₁ ps₂ : Set P} (hs : ps₁ ⊆ ps₂) (h : Concyclic ps₂) : Concyclic ps₁\n    If $S$ and $T$ are sets of points in a Euclidean space with $S \\subseteq T$ and $T$ is concyclic (i.e., all points of $T$ lie on a circle), then $S$ is also concyclic.\n[3] EuclideanGeometry.Concyclic.rec  (recursor)\n    {V : Type u_1} →\n  {P : Type u_2} →\n    [inst : NormedAddCommGroup V] →\n      [inst_1 : NormedSpace ℝ V] →\n        [inst_2 : MetricSpace P] →\n          [inst_3 : NormedAddTorsor V P] →\n            {ps : Set P} →\n              {motive : EuclideanGeometry.Concyclic ps → Sort u} →\n                ((Cospherical : EuclideanGeometry.Cospherical ps) → (Coplanar : Coplanar ℝ ps) → motive ⋯) →\n                  (t : EuclideanGeometry.Concyclic ps) → motive t\n    For any type family over concyclic sets, if we can construct an element of the type for a set that is both cospherical and coplanar, then we can extend this construction to any concyclic set using the given data.\n[4] EuclideanGeometry.mul_dist_add_mul_dist_eq_mul_dist_of_cospherical  (theorem)\n    {a b c d p : P} (h : Cospherical ({ a, b, c, d } : Set P)) (hapc : ∠ a p c = π) (hbpd : ∠ b p d = π) :\n  dist a b * dist c d + dist b c * dist d a = dist a c * dist b d\n    Given four cospherical points $a, b, c, d$ in a metric space and a point $p$ such that the angles $\\angle apc$ and $\\angle bpd$ are equal to $\\pi$, the following equality holds: $\\text{dist}(a, b) \\cdot \\text{dist}(c, d) + \\text{dist}(b, c) \\cdot \\text{dist}(d, a) = \\text{dist}(a, c) \\cdot \\text{dist}(b, d)$.\n[5] EuclideanGeometry.Concyclic.mk  (constructor)\n    ∀ {V : Type u_1} {P : Type u_2} [inst : NormedAddCommGroup V] [inst_1 : NormedSpace ℝ V] [inst_2 : MetricSpace P]\n  [inst_3 : NormedAddTorsor V P] {ps : Set P},\n  EuclideanGeometry.Cospherical ps → Coplanar ℝ ps → EuclideanGeometry.Concyclic ps\n    If a set of points in a Euclidean space is cospherical (lies on a sphere) and coplanar (lies in a plane), then the points are concyclic (lie on a circle).\n[6] EuclideanGeometry.concyclic_of_two_zsmul_oangle_eq_of_not_collinear  (theorem)\n    {p₁ p₂ p₃ p₄ : P} (h : (2 : ℤ) • ∡ p₁ p₂ p₄ = (2 : ℤ) • ∡ p₁ p₃ p₄) (hn : ¬Collinear ℝ ({ p₁, p₂, p₄ } : Set P)) :\n  Concyclic ({ p₁, p₂, p₃, p₄ } : Set P)\n    If twice the oriented angle at $p_2$ between $p_1$ and $p_4$ equals twice the oriented angle at $p_3$ between $p_1$ and $p_4$, and the points $p_1, p_2, p_4$ are not collinear, then the four points $p_1, p_2, p_3, p_4$ lie on a circle.\n[7] EuclideanGeometry.Concyclic.recOn  (definition)\n    {V : Type u_1} →\n  {P : Type u_2} →\n    [inst : NormedAddCommGroup V] →\n      [inst_1 : NormedSpace ℝ V] →\n        [inst_2 : MetricSpace P] →\n          [inst_3 : NormedAddTorsor V P] →\n            {ps : Set P} →\n              {motive : EuclideanGeometry.Concyclic ps → Sort u} →\n                (t : EuclideanGeometry.Concyclic ps) →\n                  ((Cospherical : EuclideanGeometry.Cospherical ps) → (Coplanar : Coplanar ℝ ps) → motive ⋯) → motive t\n    For any type family over concyclic sets, if we can construct an element of the type for a set that is both cospherical and coplanar, then we can extend this construction to any concyclic set using the given data.\n[8] EuclideanGeometry.angle_eq_angle_of_angle_eq_pi_of_angle_eq_pi  (theorem)\n    {p₁ p₂ p₃ p₄ p₅ : P} (hapc : ∠ p₁ p₅ p₃ = π) (hbpd : ∠ p₂ p₅ p₄ = π) : ∠ p₁ p₅ p₂ = ∠ p₃ p₅ p₄\n    If the angles ∠p₁p₅p₃ and ∠p₂p₅p₄ at point p₅ are both equal to π (straight angles), then the angles ∠p₁p₅p₂ and ∠p₃p₅p₄ formed at p₅ are equal, i.e., ∠p₁p₅p₂ = ∠p₃p₅p₄.\n</tool_response>\nLooking at the search results, I can see that `EuclideanGeometry.Concyclic` is the appropriate predicate for points lying on a circle in Mathlib.\n\nHowever, the informal statement contains an unclear condition \"$BCCS$\" which appears to be a typo. Without clarification on what this condition means, I cannot fully formalize the complete problem. I'll formalize what is clear: a pentagon $ABCDE$ inscribed in a circle (i.e., the five vertices are concyclic).\n\n```lean4\nimport Mathlib\n\nopen EuclideanGeometry\n\n/-- \nA pentagon ABCDE inscribed in a circle.\nNote: The original problem statement contains \"BCCS\" which appears to be a typo.\nThis formalization captures the concyclic condition for the pentagon vertices.\n-/\ntheorem pentagon_inscribed_in_circle \n  {V : Type*} [NormedAddCommGroup V] [NormedSpace ℝ V] \n  {P : Type*} [MetricSpace P] [NormedAddTorsor V P]\n  (A B C D E : P) \n  (h_concyclic : Concyclic ({A, B, C, D, E} : Set P)) :\n  Concyclic ({A, B, C, D, E} : Set P) := by sorry\n```\n\n# Round 1 (Semantic Self-validation)\n\nYour brief analysis:\n1. Math Assertion Analysis: The problem states a pentagon ABCDE is inscribed in a circle (i.e., all vertices lie on a circle). The \"B C C S\" is noted